        "raw": "\nOUTPUT\n1) FORMAL RESTATEMENT\nA set $A\\subseteq\\mathbb N$ is an \\emph{additive complement to the primes} if every sufficiently large integer $n$\ncan be written as\n\\[n=p+a\\quad\\text{with $p$ prime and $a\\in A$.}\\]\nQuestions (as stated):\n(a) Can one choose such an $A$ with $|A\\cap[1,N]|=o((\\log N)^2)$?\n(b) Can one achieve $|A\\cap[1,N]|=O(\\log N)$?\n(c) Must any such $A$ satisfy $\\liminf_{N\\to\\infty} |A\\cap[1,N]|/\\log N >1$?\n\n2) QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK\nBrowsing is not available. I only record what the problem statement itself claims.\nI have not verified those results here.\n\n3) ATTACK PLAN\nProof track:\n1. Use probabilistic method: choose $A$ randomly with density about $(\\log N)^2/N$ on dyadic intervals and show coverage.\n2. Prove lower bounds on $|A\\cap[1,N]|$ by a counting argument using $\\pi(N)$.\n\nDisproof track:\n1. Search for congruence obstructions preventing $O(\\log N)$ complements.\n\nChosen path: prove an unconditional counting lower bound in terms of $\\pi(N)$.\n\n4) WORK\nLemma 1 (Counting lower bound via prime counting).\nAssume every integer $n$ with $1\\le n\\le N$ can be written as $n=p+a$ with $p$ prime and $a\\in A$.\nLet $m(N):=|A\\cap[1,N]|$ and let $\\pi(N)$ be the number of primes $\\le N$. Then\n\\[m(N)\\,\\pi(N)\\ge N.\n\\]\n\nProof.\nFor each $a\\in A\\cap[1,N]$, the integers representable as $p+a$ with $p\\le N$ form a set of size at most $\\pi(N)$.\nThus the total number of integers in $[1,N]$ that can be covered using shifts by elements of $A\\cap[1,N]$ is at most\n$m(N)\\pi(N)$ (union bound). Since by hypothesis all $N$ integers are covered, we get $m(N)\\pi(N)\\ge N$. \\qed\n\nCorollary 2 (Conditional logarithmic lower bound).\nAssuming the prime number theorem estimate $\\pi(N)\\sim N/\\log N$, Lemma 1 implies\n\\[m(N)\\ge (1+o(1))\\log N.\n\\]\n\nProof.\nFrom Lemma 1, $m(N)\\ge N/\\pi(N)$; substitute $\\pi(N)\\sim N/\\log N$. \\qed\n\n5) VERIFICATION\n- Lemma 1 is a coarse union bound and ignores overlaps; it is still valid.\n- Corollary 2 depends on the prime number theorem, which is not proved here.\n\n6) FINAL\n**UNRESOLVED**\n\n(i) Strongest fully proved partial result:\nA necessary condition for covering $[1,N]$ is $|A\\cap[1,N]|\\ge N/\\pi(N)$ (Lemma 1).\n\n(ii) First gap:\nNo construction here achieves $|A\\cap[1,N]|=o((\\log N)^2)$, and no unconditional lower bound of order $\\log N$ is proved\nwithout using deep estimates for $\\pi(N)$.\n\n(iii) Top 3 next moves:\n1. Implement Erd\\H{o}s's probabilistic construction (as described in the problem statement) in a self-contained way.\n2. Strengthen Lemma 1 by incorporating overlaps (e.g. via inclusion--exclusion or sieve) to approach the constant $e^\\gamma$ bound.\n3. Investigate whether $O(\\log N)$ is possible by building explicit near-optimal complements.\n\n(iv) Minimal counterexample structure (if $O(\\log N)$ were impossible):\nAn obstruction showing that for infinitely many $N$, the shifts $\\mathcal P + a$ (for $a\\in A\\cap[1,N]$) cannot cover\n$[1,N]$ unless $|A\\cap[1,N]|$ exceeds $C\\log N$ for some $C>0$.\n",
