          "WORK": "Lemma 1 ($B_3$ implies Sidon for infinite sets).\nLet $A\\subset\\mathbb N$ be infinite and satisfy the $B_3$ condition above. Then $A$ is a Sidon set (a $B_2$ set):\nif $a,b,c,d\\in A$ with $a\\le b$, $c\\le d$ and $a+b=c+d$, then $\\{a,b\\}=\\{c,d\\}$.\n\nProof.\nAssume $a+b=c+d$ with $\\{a,b\\}\\ne\\{c,d\\}$. Since $A$ is infinite, choose $e\\in A$ with $e\\notin\\{a,b,c,d\\}$.\nThen\n\\[a+b+e=c+d+e.\\]\nApply the $B_3$ property to the triples $(a,b,e)$ and $(c,d,e)$ (after sorting each nondecreasingly).\nBecause $e$ occurs in both triples and $e\\notin\\{a,b,c,d\\}$, equality of the multisets $\\{a,b,e\\}=\\{c,d,e\\}$\nforces $\\{a,b\\}=\\{c,d\\}$, contradicting the assumption. \\qed\n\nLemma 2 (Counting bound in $[1,N]$).\nLet $N\\ge 1$, and let $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ be a finite set such that all sums $x+y+z$ with $x\\le y\\le z$ in $S$\nare distinct. Writing $k:=|S|$, one has\n\\[\\binom{k+2}{3}\\le 3N-2,\\]\nand hence $k \\ll N^{1/3}$ (e.g. $k\\le (18N)^{1/3}+2$).\n\nProof.\nThe number of multisets of size $3$ chosen from a $k$-element set is $\\binom{k+2}{3}$, and these correspond exactly to\nthe triples $x\\le y\\le z$ in $S$.\nBy assumption, all the sums $x+y+z$ for such triples are distinct integers.\nMoreover, since $1\\le x,y,z\\le N$, each such sum lies in the interval $\\{3,4,\\dots,3N\\}$, which has size $3N-2$.\nTherefore the number of such triples is at most $3N-2$, i.e. $\\binom{k+2}{3}\\le 3N-2$.\nThe displayed explicit bound on $k$ follows from $\\binom{k+2}{3}=\\frac{k(k+1)(k+2)}{6}\\ge \\frac{k^3}{6}$. \\qed\n\nFAST REALITY CHECK (local computation).\nI generated the greedy $B_3$ sequence (start with $\\emptyset$ and repeatedly adjoin the smallest integer preserving the $B_3$ condition).\nFirst 18 terms:\n\\[1,2,5,14,33,72,125,219,376,573,745,1209,1557,2442,3098,4048,5298,6704.\\]\nCounts and ratios (using this finite prefix as a proxy):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nN & A(N) & A(N)/N^{1/3} \\\\\\hline\n100 & 6 & 1.29\\\\\n1000 & 11 & 1.10\\\\\n10000 & 18 & 0.84\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThis suggests the ratio might drift downward in the greedy construction, but this is far from evidence for the liminf statement.",
