        "raw": "\n\\section{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#151 (Round 2): a new obstruction via common neighbourhoods}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\nFor Problem \\#151 I pursue path (C): I do not have a full proof or a counterexample, so the correct Round-2 goal is to isolate a \\emph{sharp structural obstruction} that any counterexample would have to satisfy.\n\nRound~1 already proved the general inequality\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-\\alpha(G),\n\\]\nwhere $\\alpha(G)$ is the independence number. The new Round-2 goal is to add a second, genuinely different mechanism that also forces small clique transversals.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following Round-1 facts.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The Round-1 lemma\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-\\alpha(G).\n\\]\n\\item The Round-1 proof that the conjecture holds trivially for triangle-free graphs.\n\\item The Round-1 observation that the general bound $\\tau(G)\\le n-\\sqrt n$ is easy but much weaker than the target $\\tau(G)\\le n-H(n)$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND 2)}\nThe new tool is a codegree lemma.\n\nFor an edge $uv\\in E(G)$, let\n\\[\n\\mathrm{codeg}(u,v):=|N(u)\\cap N(v)|.\n\\]\nThe new observation is that\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-\\mathrm{codeg}(u,v)\n\\]\nfor \\emph{every} edge $uv$.\n\nThis is different in nature from the Round-1 bound involving $\\alpha(G)$: it uses a dense local configuration (a large common neighbourhood of one edge), not a sparse global configuration (a large independent set).\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND 2)}\nThe exact gap after Round~1 was that we only had one broad sufficient condition for the conjecture:\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)\\ge H(n)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau(G)\\le n-H(n).\n\\]\nI will prove a second broad sufficient condition:\n\\[\n\\exists\\, uv\\in E(G)\\mbox{ with }|N(u)\\cap N(v)|\\ge H(n)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\tau(G)\\le n-H(n).\n\\]\n\nCombining the two will give a structural obstruction:\nevery counterexample must satisfy simultaneously\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)<H(n)\n\\quad\\mbox{and}\\quad\n|N(u)\\cap N(v)|<H(n)\\ \\mbox{for every edge }uv.\n\\]\nIn particular, every counterexample must also satisfy\n\\[\n\\omega(G)\\le H(n)+1,\n\\]\nbecause any clique of size $r$ gives an edge with at least $r-2$ common neighbours.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND 2)}\n\n\\paragraph{Lemma (common-neighbourhood transversal).}\nFor every graph $G$ on $n$ vertices and every edge $uv\\in E(G)$,\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-|N(u)\\cap N(v)|.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nFix an edge $uv$. Let\n\\[\nS:=N(u)\\cap N(v),\n\\qquad\nT:=V(G)\\setminus S.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|T|=n-|S|=n-|N(u)\\cap N(v)|.\n\\]\nI claim that $T$ is a clique transversal.\n\nSuppose not. Then there exists a maximal clique $C$ of size at least $2$ with\n\\[\nC\\cap T=\\emptyset.\n\\]\nSo $C\\subseteq S=N(u)\\cap N(v)$. In particular, every vertex of $C$ is adjacent to $u$. Therefore\n\\[\nC\\cup\\{u\\}\n\\]\nis a clique strictly larger than $C$, contradicting the maximality of $C$.\n\nSo $T$ is indeed a clique transversal, and hence\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le |T|=n-|N(u)\\cap N(v)|.\n\\]\n\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.}\nIf $G$ has an edge $uv$ such that\n\\[\n|N(u)\\cap N(v)|\\ge H(n),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-H(n).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nApply the lemma to that edge.\n\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 2.}\nIf\n\\[\n\\max\\Bigl\\{\\alpha(G),\\ \\max_{uv\\in E(G)} |N(u)\\cap N(v)|\\Bigr\\}\\ge H(n),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-H(n).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nIf $\\alpha(G)\\ge H(n)$, use the Round-1 bound $\\tau(G)\\le n-\\alpha(G)$. Otherwise, if some edge has common neighbourhood size at least $H(n)$, use Corollary~1.\n\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 3 (clique obstruction).}\nIf $G$ is a counterexample to the conjectured inequality\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-H(n),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\omega(G)\\le H(n)+1.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nSuppose $\\omega(G)\\ge H(n)+2$, and let $K$ be a clique of that size. Pick any edge $uv$ of $K$. Then every vertex of $K\\setminus\\{u,v\\}$ lies in $N(u)\\cap N(v)$, so\n\\[\n|N(u)\\cap N(v)|\\ge |K|-2\\ge H(n).\n\\]\nBy Corollary~1 the conjectured inequality would then hold, contradiction.\n\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 4 (triangle-count version).}\nLet $t(G)$ denote the number of triangles of $G$ and $e(G)$ the number of edges. Then\n\\[\n\\tau(G)\\le n-\\left\\lceil \\frac{3t(G)}{e(G)}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\nwhenever $e(G)>0$.\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nThe average value of $|N(u)\\cap N(v)|$ over all edges $uv$ equals\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{e(G)}\\sum_{uv\\in E(G)} |N(u)\\cap N(v)|\n=\n\\frac{3t(G)}{e(G)},\n\\]\nsince each triangle contributes $1$ to the codegree of each of its $3$ edges. Therefore some edge $uv$ satisfies\n\\[\n|N(u)\\cap N(v)|\\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{3t(G)}{e(G)}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nNow apply the lemma.\n\n\n\\paragraph{Round-2 structural obstruction.}\nCombining Round~1 with Corollary~2, any counterexample $G$ on $n$ vertices must satisfy\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)<H(n)\n\\quad\\mbox{and}\\quad\n|N(u)\\cap N(v)|<H(n)\\ \\mbox{for every edge }uv.\n\\]\nEquivalently, every counterexample must simultaneously have:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item no independent set of size $H(n)$, and\n\\item no edge lying in $H(n)$ or more triangles.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThis sharply narrows the possible shape of a counterexample.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\nI check the possible pitfalls.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Why does a clique contained in $N(u)\\cap N(v)$ contradict maximality?} Because every vertex of that clique is adjacent to $u$, so adjoining $u$ enlarges the clique. There is no need to use both $u$ and $v$.\n\\item \\textbf{Does the lemma fail for isolated edges or sparse graphs?} No. If $N(u)\\cap N(v)=\\emptyset$, the lemma simply gives the trivial estimate $\\tau(G)\\le n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Does Corollary 3 use more than is proved?} No. A clique of size $r$ gives an edge with at least $r-2$ common neighbours, and that is exactly what the lemma needs.\n\\item \\textbf{Is the triangle-count version correct?} Yes. Each triangle contributes to the common-neighbour count of exactly its three edges, so the averaging identity is exact.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nRound~2 does not prove the Erd\\H{o}s--Gallai--Tuza conjecture, but it adds a genuinely new sufficient condition and a sharp obstruction:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Round~1 showed the conjecture whenever $\\alpha(G)\\ge H(n)$.\n\\item Round~2 now also shows the conjecture whenever some edge has at least $H(n)$ common neighbours.\n\\item Therefore any counterexample must have both\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)<H(n)\n\\quad\\mbox{and}\\quad\n\\max_{uv\\in E(G)}|N(u)\\cap N(v)|<H(n),\n\\]\nand in particular\n\\[\n\\omega(G)\\le H(n)+1.\n\\]\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThis is a real narrowing of the search space for counterexamples and a clear structural advance beyond Round~1.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 58\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Erd\\H{o}s Problems, Problem \\#151, checked on 7 March 2026.\n\\item Erd\\H{o}s Problems, Problem \\#610 (for the currently recorded general bound $\\tau(G)\\le n-\\sqrt{2n}+O(1)$), checked on 7 March 2026.\n\\end{enumerate}\n",
