        "status": "unresolved",
        "raw": "\nPROBLEM\n\nA finite set $A\\subset \\mathbb{R}^n$ is called Ramsey if, for any $k\\geq 1$, there exists some $d=d(A,k)$ such that in any $k$-colouring of $\\mathbb{R}^d$ there exists a monochromatic copy of $A$. Characterise the Ramsey sets in $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nOUTPUT\n\n## 1) FORMAL RESTATEMENT\n\nFix $n\\ge 1$.\nA finite set $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ is called **Ramsey** if for every integer $k\\ge 1$ there exists an integer $d=d(A,k)\\ge 1$ such that for every coloring\n\\[\nc:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}\n\\]\nthere exists an isometric embedding (distance-preserving map)\n\\[\n\\varphi:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^d\n\\]\nand a color $\\gamma$ such that $c$ is constant equal to $\\gamma$ on $\\varphi(A)$.\n\nThe problem asks for a characterization of such finite sets $A$.\n\n---\n\n## 2) QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK\n\nI will not use or claim any external results beyond what is explicitly written in the problem text.\n\nThe problem text reports:\n\n* Every Ramsey set is spherical (lies on a sphere).\n* Two conjectures: spherical $\\Rightarrow$ Ramsey (Graham), and Ramsey $\\Leftrightarrow$ subtransitive (Leader\u2013Russell\u2013Walters).\n* Several families known to be Ramsey (rectangles, simplices, trapezoids, regular polygons/polyhedra).\n\nI do not re-prove these deep results.\n\n---\n\n## 3) ATTACK PLAN\n\nA full characterization is beyond my current reach. I instead prove basic closure properties that any proposed characterization must respect.\n\n---\n\n## 4) WORK\n\n### Lemma 4.1 (congruence invariance)\n\nIf $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ is Ramsey and $A'$ is congruent to $A$ (i.e. $A'=\\psi(A)$ for some isometry $\\psi$ of $\\mathbb R^n$), then $A'$ is Ramsey.\n\n**Proof.**\nFix $k\\ge 1$.\nSince $A$ is Ramsey, there exists $d=d(A,k)$ such that every $k$-coloring of $\\mathbb R^d$ contains a monochromatic copy of $A$.\n\nLet $c:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$ be any $k$-coloring.\nBy the Ramsey property of $A$, there exists an isometric embedding $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^d$ such that $c$ is constant on $\\varphi(A)$.\n\nNow consider $A'=\\psi(A)$. The composition $\\varphi\\circ \\psi^{-1}$ is also an isometric embedding of $\\mathbb R^n$ into $\\mathbb R^d$, and\n\\[\n(\\varphi\\circ\\psi^{-1})(A')=(\\varphi\\circ\\psi^{-1})(\\psi(A))=\\varphi(A).\n\\]\nTherefore $c$ is constant on $(\\varphi\\circ\\psi^{-1})(A')$, giving a monochromatic copy of $A'$.\nSince $c$ was arbitrary, the same $d$ works for $A'$.\n$\\square$\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 4.2 (heredity under taking subsets)\n\nIf $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ is Ramsey and $B\\subseteq A$ is a (finite) subset, then $B$ is Ramsey.\n\n**Proof.**\nFix $k\\ge 1$ and let $d=d(A,k)$ witness that $A$ is Ramsey for $k$ colors.\nGiven any $k$-coloring $c$ of $\\mathbb R^d$, there exists an isometric embedding $\\varphi$ such that $c$ is constant on $\\varphi(A)$.\nThen in particular $c$ is constant on $\\varphi(B)\\subseteq \\varphi(A)$, so $\\varphi(B)$ is a monochromatic copy of $B$.\nThus the same $d$ works for $B$.\n$\\square$\n\n---\n\n## 5) VERIFICATION\n\n* Lemma 4.1: the only step is that composition with an isometry preserves distances, so it maps congruent copies to congruent copies.\n* Lemma 4.2: immediate from the definition; any monochromatic copy of $A$ contains a monochromatic copy of any subset.\n\nThese lemmas are correct but do not approach the deep classification problem.\n\n---\n\n## 6) FINAL\n\n**UNRESOLVED**\n\n(i) **Strongest fully proved partial result obtained here.**\n\nI proved two structural necessities for the class of Ramsey sets:\n\n* invariance under congruence (Lemma 4.1);\n* heredity under taking subsets (Lemma 4.2).\n\n(ii) **Exact first gap.**\n\nGive a nontrivial necessary-and-sufficient geometric characterization of Ramsey sets beyond these basic closure properties.\n\n(iii) **Top 3 next moves (concrete targets).**\n\n1. Prove \u201cspherical $\\Rightarrow$ Ramsey\u201d or find a spherical counterexample.\n2. Understand the \u201csubtransitive\u201d condition and either prove it implies Ramsey or find a Ramsey set that is not subtransitive.\n3. Develop obstructions: show that certain non-spherical configurations cannot be Ramsey by constructing explicit colorings avoiding them.\n\n(iv) **What a minimal counterexample would likely look like.**\n\nA minimal counterexample to \u201cspherical $\\Rightarrow$ Ramsey\u201d would be a spherical finite set $A$ with a family of colorings in increasing dimension avoiding monochromatic copies. Such a counterexample would likely be highly symmetric (to evade standard Ramsey embeddings) yet fail the subtransitivity property.\n\n\n",
