        "sections": {
          "preamble": "\\section{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#208}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\n\\textbf{Path pursued: (A) full proof of the Round~1 target statement.}\n\nRound~1 treated the statement\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}),\n\\]\nwhere $s_1<s_2<\\cdots$ is the increasing sequence of squarefree integers, as unresolved and only obtained the much weaker bound $s_{n+1}-s_n\\ll \\sqrt{s_n}$.\n\nThe decisive new fact is that the Round~1 target statement is now a theorem: Pandey proved a fixed exponent improvement beyond $1/5$. Therefore the correct Round~2 objective is to convert that theorem into a short, gap-free proof of the original statement.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\n\nI use only the following Round~1 items.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The definition of the sequence $s_1<s_2<\\cdots$ of squarefree integers.\n\\item The precise target statement from Round~1:\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}).\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\n\nI do \\emph{not} reuse the Round~1 proof of the weaker bound $s_{n+1}-s_n\\ll \\sqrt{s_n}$.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe new tool is Pandey's 2024 theorem:\n\n\\medskip\n\n\\emph{There exists $\\eta>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $X$, the interval}\n\\[\n[X,\\ X+X^{1/5-\\eta}]\n\\]\n\\emph{contains a squarefree integer.}\n\n\\medskip\n\nThis is strictly stronger than the original Round~1 target. Indeed, it immediately implies\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n\\ll s_n^{1/5-\\eta},\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}).\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe Round~1 gap was simple: no theorem beyond the exponent $1/5$ was available inside the write-up.\n\nTo close that gap, I will:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item state Pandey's theorem precisely;\n\\item substitute $X=s_n+1$ (not $X=s_n$);\n\\item deduce a direct upper bound for $s_{n+1}-s_n$;\n\\item divide by $s_n^{1/5}$ and let $n\\to \\infty$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThe choice $X=s_n+1$ is important: if one took $X=s_n$, the guaranteed squarefree integer in $[X,X+X^{1/5-\\eta}]$ could be the left endpoint $s_n$ itself, which would not control the next gap. Shifting by $1$ avoids that issue completely.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\n\\paragraph{External theorem used (Pandey).}\nThere exists a constant $\\eta>0$ such that for all sufficiently large real numbers $X$, the interval\n\\[\n[X,\\ X+X^{1/5-\\eta}]\n\\]\ncontains at least one squarefree integer.\n\n\\paragraph{Theorem 5.1.}\nFor the increasing sequence $s_1<s_2<\\cdots$ of squarefree integers, one has\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nLet $\\eta>0$ be as in Pandey's theorem. Fix $n$ large enough that $X:=s_n+1$ lies in the range where Pandey's theorem applies. Then the interval\n\\[\n[s_n+1,\\ s_n+1+(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}]\n\\]\ncontains a squarefree integer.\n\nLet $t$ be such a squarefree integer. Since $t\\ge s_n+1$, we have $t>s_n$. By definition, $s_{n+1}$ is the least squarefree integer strictly larger than $s_n$, so\n\\[\ns_{n+1}\\le t\\le s_n+1+(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n\\le 1+(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}.\n\\]\nNow divide by $s_n^{1/5}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{s_{n+1}-s_n}{s_n^{1/5}}\n\\le\n\\frac{1}{s_n^{1/5}}\n+\n\\frac{(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}}{s_n^{1/5}}.\n\\]\nAs $n\\to\\infty$, we have $s_n\\to\\infty$, so\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{s_n^{1/5}}\\to 0,\n\\]\nand also\n\\[\n\\frac{(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}}{s_n^{1/5}}\n=\n\\left(1+\\frac{1}{s_n}\\right)^{1/5-\\eta}s_n^{-\\eta}\\to 0.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac{s_{n+1}-s_n}{s_n^{1/5}}\\to 0,\n\\]\nwhich is exactly\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}).\n\\]\n\\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{What this does and does not settle.}\nThe Round~1 target statement is therefore solved affirmatively. However, the \\emph{current stronger formulation} recorded on the Erd\\H{o}s problem page asks whether\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n\\ll_\\varepsilon s_n^\\varepsilon\n\\qquad\\text{for every }\\varepsilon>0,\n\\]\nwhich is much stronger. Pandey's theorem gives a single fixed exponent $1/5-\\eta$, not arbitrary $\\varepsilon$. So the original Round~1 target is solved, while the stronger modern formulation remains open.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\n\\paragraph{(i) Why was $X=s_n+1$ chosen instead of $X=s_n$?}\nBecause if $X=s_n$, Pandey's theorem only guarantees a squarefree number somewhere in\n\\[\n[s_n,\\ s_n+s_n^{1/5-\\eta}],\n\\]\nand that number could be $s_n$ itself. Then no bound on $s_{n+1}-s_n$ would follow. The shift by $1$ removes this possibility.\n\n\\paragraph{(ii) Does Pandey's theorem apply to integer $X$ only?}\nThe statement is formulated for sufficiently large $X$. Taking $X=s_n+1$ is legitimate since $s_n+1\\to\\infty$.\n\n\\paragraph{(iii) Is the deduction from the existence of $t$ to the bound on $s_{n+1}$ valid?}\nYes. By definition, $s_{n+1}$ is the smallest squarefree integer greater than $s_n$, and $t$ is one such squarefree integer. Therefore $s_{n+1}\\le t$.\n\n\\paragraph{(iv) Do the limits really give little-$o$?}\nYes. Both terms on the right-hand side after division by $s_n^{1/5}$ tend to $0$, so the whole quotient tends to $0$.\n\n\\paragraph{(v) Any hidden issue with endpoint conventions?}\nNo. Since $t\\ge s_n+1$, we have $t>s_n$ regardless of whether the interval is interpreted with closed or half-open endpoints at the left.\n\nThe proof is therefore gap-free.\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL (EXACTLY ONE)}\n\n\\textbf{FULL SOLUTION --- FULL PROOF.}\n\nPandey's theorem implies\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n\\le 1+(s_n+1)^{1/5-\\eta}\n\\]\nfor some fixed $\\eta>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, and hence\n\\[\ns_{n+1}-s_n=o(s_n^{1/5}).\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE (MANDATORY)}\n\n\\textbf{COMPLETION: 100\\%}\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item M. Pandey, \\emph{Squarefree numbers in short intervals}, arXiv:2401.13981, 2024.\n\\item T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#208}, \\texttt{erdosproblems.com}, accessed 2026-03-07.\n\\end{enumerate}"
        },
