          "preamble": "\\section*{Problem 222 --- Round 2}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\nPath \\textbf{(C): obstruction/correction}.  The original prompt ``explore the behaviour'' is too broad to finish in one round.  The sharpest Round~1 gap was the very weak pointwise upper bound $\\Delta_k\\le 2\\sqrt{n_k}+1$.  In this round I correct that to the classical Bambah--Chowla scale and give a complete proof:\n\\[\n\\Delta_k \\le 2\\sqrt{2}\\,(n_k+1)^{1/4}+2.\n\\]\nThis is a major quantitative improvement from exponent $1/2$ down to exponent $1/4$.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the Round~1 notation\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{n\\ge 0:n=a^2+b^2\\mbox{ for some integers }a,b\\},\n\\qquad\n0\\le n_1<n_2<\\cdots,\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_k=n_{k+1}-n_k.\n\\]\nI also retain the Round~1 theorem that the gaps are unbounded (via the CRT construction), though that theorem is not reproved here.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe new tool is the arithmetic form of the Bambah--Chowla argument: for a given real $x\\ge 1$, choose\n\\[\nu:=\\lfloor \\sqrt{x}\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\nv:=\\left\\lceil \\sqrt{x-u^2}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThen $u^2+v^2$ is a sum of two squares lying very close above $x$, and a direct estimate shows the error is $O(x^{1/4})$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe missing claim after Round~1 was a strong universal upper bound for the next representable number.  So the precise target is:\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Claim.} For every real $x\\ge 1$ there exists $m\\in\\mathcal S$ with\n\\[\nx\\le m < x+2\\sqrt{2}\\,x^{1/4}+1.\n\\]\n\nOnce this is proved, applying it with $x=n_k+1$ gives a pointwise bound for the consecutive gap $\\Delta_k$.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\nLet $x\\ge 1$ be real, and define\n\\[\nu:=\\lfloor\\sqrt{x}\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\nv:=\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{x-u^2}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThen $u,v$ are nonnegative integers, $u^2\\le x$, and therefore $x-u^2\\ge 0$.\nBy the defining property of the ceiling function,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x-u^2}\\le v < \\sqrt{x-u^2}+1.\n\\]\nSquaring gives\n\\[\nx-u^2\\le v^2 < x-u^2 + 2\\sqrt{x-u^2}+1.\n\\]\nAfter adding $u^2$ to all three terms we obtain\n\\[\nx\\le u^2+v^2 < x + 2\\sqrt{x-u^2}+1.\n\\]\nSo it remains to estimate $x-u^2$.\nSince $u=\\lfloor\\sqrt{x}\\rfloor$, we have $u\\ge \\sqrt{x}-1$, hence\n\\[\nu^2 \\ge (\\sqrt{x}-1)^2 = x-2\\sqrt{x}+1.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nx-u^2 \\le 2\\sqrt{x}-1.\n\\]\nSubstituting this into the previous inequality yields\n\\[\nu^2+v^2 < x+2\\sqrt{2\\sqrt{x}-1}+1 < x+2\\sqrt{2}\\,x^{1/4}+1.\n\\]\nBecause $u^2+v^2\\in\\mathcal S$, this proves the claim.\n\nNow take $x=n_k+1$.  Then there exists $m\\in\\mathcal S$ with\n\\[\nn_k+1\\le m < n_k+1+2\\sqrt{2}\\,(n_k+1)^{1/4}+1.\n\\]\nSince $n_{k+1}$ is the least element of $\\mathcal S$ strictly larger than $n_k$, we have $n_{k+1}\\le m$.  Hence\n\\[\n\\Delta_k=n_{k+1}-n_k\\le 2\\sqrt{2}\\,(n_k+1)^{1/4}+2.\n\\]\nThus Round~2 proves the following corrected theorem.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Corrected theorem proved in Round~2.}\nFor every $k\\ge 1$,\n\\[\n\\Delta_k \\le 2\\sqrt{2}\\,(n_k+1)^{1/4}+2.\n\\]\nEquivalently, the maximal gap function among sums of two squares satisfies\n\\[\ng(X):=\\max\\{n_{j+1}-n_j:n_j\\le X\\}\\ll X^{1/4}.\n\\]\nTogether with the Round~1 CRT construction, this gives the rigorous window\n\\[\ng(X)\\to\\infty\n\\qquad\\mbox{and}\\qquad\ng(X)\\ll X^{1/4}.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{Is $x-u^2$ always nonnegative?} Yes, because $u=\\lfloor\\sqrt{x}\\rfloor$ implies $u^2\\le x$.\n\\item \\emph{Could the constructed sum of two squares equal $n_k$ when deriving the gap bound?} That is why the theorem is applied to $x=n_k+1$, not to $x=n_k$.\n\\item \\emph{Any issue at small $x$?} No.  For example $x=1$ gives $u=1$, $v=0$, and the inequality is immediate.\n\\item \\emph{Did the exponent really drop from $1/2$ to $1/4$?} Yes: the only nontrivial estimate is $x-u^2\\le 2\\sqrt{x}-1$, and taking one more square root produces the exponent $1/4$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}  The overall behaviour of gaps between sums of two squares is still not fully understood, but Round~2 proves a sharp classical pointwise upper bound absent from Round~1:\n\\[\n\\Delta_k \\le 2\\sqrt{2}\\,(n_k+1)^{1/4}+2.\n\\]\nThis closes the main quantitative gap in the Round~1 work.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 65\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item R. P. Bambah and S. Chowla, \\emph{On numbers which can be expressed as a sum of two squares}, Proc. Nat. Inst. Sci. India \\textbf{13} (1947), 101--103.\n\\item P. Shiu, \\emph{On a result of Bambah and Chowla}, Integers \\textbf{19} (2019), A48.\n\\end{itemize}"
