          "preamble": "\\section*{Problem \\#263}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Problem \\#263}\n\n\\subsection*{1. Formal Restatement}\nCall a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers $(a_n)_{n\\ge 1}$ a \\emph{Type-2 irrationality sequence} if for every integer sequence $(b_n)_{n\\ge 1}$ with\n\\[\n\\frac{b_n}{a_n}\\to 1\\qquad (n\\to\\infty),\n\\]\nthe series $\\sum_{n\\ge 1} 1/b_n$ is irrational (assuming the sum converges, which it does if $b_n\\to\\infty$ sufficiently fast).\n\nQuestions:\n\\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*),leftmargin=2em]\n\\item Is $a_n=2^{2^n}$ a Type-2 irrationality sequence?\n\\item Must every Type-2 irrationality sequence satisfy $a_n^{1/n}\\to\\infty$?\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{2. Quick Literature / Context Check}\nA ``folklore'' sufficient condition states that $\\sum 1/a_n$ is irrational if $\\lim a_n^{1/2^n}=\\infty$.\nRecent work of Kova\\v{c} and Tao studies several ``types'' of irrationality sequences; in particular they show a broad class of sequences are \\emph{not} Type-2 when $a_{n+1}/a_n^2\\to 0$ (under convergence of $\\sum 1/a_n$), while the folklore condition yields Type-2 examples under the much stronger growth $\\liminf a_{n+1}/a_n^{2+\\varepsilon}>0$.\nThe borderline case $a_{n+1}\\asymp a_n^2$ includes $a_n=2^{2^n}$ (where $a_{n+1}=a_n^2$) and remains open.\n\n\\subsection*{3. Attack Plan}\nTwo natural directions:\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item \\textbf{Try to prove Type-2 for $2^{2^n}$.} One would need a digit/carry or Diophantine approximation argument showing that any perturbation $b_n=2^{2^n}(1+o(1))$ forces the sum $\\sum 1/b_n$ to avoid all rationals.\n\\item \\textbf{Try to disprove Type-2 for $2^{2^n}$.} Construct a specific integer sequence $b_n$ with $b_n/2^{2^n}\\to 1$ such that $\\sum 1/b_n\\in\\mathbb{Q}$. This resembles building an ``Egyptian-fraction-like'' expansion with very large denominators constrained to lie in thin relative intervals.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{4. Work (provable partial results)}\nWe record the known partial implications as clean lemmas (with proofs only where elementary).\n\n\\begin{lemma}[A sufficient growth condition implying Type-2]\n\\label{lem:263-sufficient}\nAssume there exists $\\varepsilon>0$ and $c>0$ such that for all sufficiently large $n$,\n\\[\n a_{n+1} \\ge c\\, a_n^{2+\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nThen $(a_n)$ is Type-2.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]\nUnder the hypothesis, $a_n$ grows faster than doubly-exponential in the sense that $a_n^{1/2^n}\\to\\infty$. If $b_n/a_n\\to 1$ then also $b_n^{1/2^n}\\to\\infty$. The folklore irrationality criterion (see the cited sources) then gives that $\\sum 1/b_n$ is irrational. Hence $(a_n)$ is Type-2.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{lemma}[A broad obstruction (Kova\\v{c}--Tao)]\n\\label{lem:263-obstruction}\nSuppose $(a_n)$ is strictly increasing, $\\sum 1/a_n$ converges, and\n\\[\n\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n^2}\\to 0.\n\\]\nThen $(a_n)$ is \\emph{not} Type-2.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\paragraph{Remark.}\nThe sequence $a_n=2^{2^n}$ satisfies $a_{n+1}/a_n^2=1$, so it lies exactly on the boundary not covered by Lemma~\\ref{lem:263-obstruction} and does not satisfy the sufficient condition in Lemma~\\ref{lem:263-sufficient}.\n\n\\subsection*{5. Verification / Sanity Checks}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item For $a_n=2^{2^n}$, indeed $a_{n+1}=2^{2^{n+1}}=(2^{2^n})^2=a_n^2$, so $a_{n+1}/a_n^2\\equiv 1$.\n\\item The ``folklore'' criterion is consistent with classical irrationality proofs for rapidly growing Egyptian-fraction denominators.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{6. FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Why stuck / what remains open}\nExisting theorems cover either much slower growth (showing \\emph{not} Type-2) or much faster growth (showing Type-2). The sequence $2^{2^n}$ is precisely the ``critical'' growth rate $a_{n+1}\\asymp a_n^2$ where current methods (per the cited references) do not decide Type-2.\n\nThe second question (whether Type-2 forces $a_n^{1/n}\\to\\infty$) also remains open; current results only give partial constraints.\n\n\\subsubsection*{Strongest partial result proved here}\nLemma~\\ref{lem:263-sufficient}: a super-quadratic recurrence $a_{n+1}\\ge c a_n^{2+\\varepsilon}$ guarantees Type-2.\n\n\\subsubsection*{Next steps (concrete)}\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]\n\\item Attempt a ``critical'' carry/approximation analysis for $a_{n+1}=a_n^2$ (or close): show that any rational approximation would force impossible congruence/size constraints on $b_n$.\n\\item Try to explicitly construct $b_n=2^{2^n}+o(2^{2^n})$ so that partial sums hit a fixed rational within an error smaller than the remaining tail.\n\\item Search for intermediate criteria between $a_{n+1}/a_n^2\\to 0$ and $a_{n+1}\\ge a_n^{2+\\varepsilon}$ that can decide the borderline.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7. Completion Estimate}\n\\textbf{35\\%}. We can cleanly state the known sufficient/obstruction regimes and locate $2^{2^n}$ at the critical boundary, but the main question is still open.\n\n\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem{Bloom263}\nT. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#263}, \\url{https://www.erdosproblems.com/263} (accessed 2026-01-17).\n\n\\bibitem{BoLo90}\nP. Borwein and L. Loring,\n\\emph{Some questions of Erd\\H{o}s and Graham on numbers of the form $\\sum g_n/2^{g_n}$},\nAmer. Math. Monthly \\textbf{97} (1990), 22--26.\n\n\\bibitem{TUZ20}\nS. Tengely, M. Ulas, and J. Zygad\\l{}o,\n\\emph{On a Diophantine equation of Erd\\H{o}s and Graham},\nRevista Colombiana de Matem\\'aticas \\textbf{54} (2020), 445--459.\n\n\\bibitem{KoTa24}\nV. Kova\\v{c} and T. Tao,\n\\emph{The Ahmes series and irrationality sequences},\narXiv:2406.17593 (version v4, 2025-07-14).\n\n\\end{thebibliography}"
