        "raw": "\n\\section*{Round 2 continuation for Erd\\H{o}s Problems \\#278, \\#279, \\#281}\n\nWe write $d(S)$ for natural density when it exists, $\\overline d(S)$ for upper density, and $\\underline d(S)$ for lower density.\n\n\\section*{Problem 279}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\nWe pursue \\textbf{(C) obstruction/correction}. Round 1 only gave heuristic density considerations. The strongest promising direction is to determine whether the unresolved issue is really one of \\emph{density} or one of \\emph{finiteness}. The new theorem below shows that density is not the obstruction at all.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\n\nWe use two Round-1 ideas.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The reformulation: an integer $m$ is covered once there is a modulus $n$ with\n\\[\nm\\equiv a_n\\pmod n\n\\]\nand with $n$ small enough relative to $m$ so that the quotient $t=(m-a_n)/n$ automatically satisfies $t\\ge k$.\n\\item For pairwise coprime moduli, finite-prefix avoidance densities factor exactly.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe new insight is that for pairwise coprime moduli, the exceptional set has upper density $0$ \\emph{for every choice of residues}, not merely for some probabilistically chosen one.\n\nIn particular, for the primes and any fixed $k\\ge 1$, the set of integers not representable as\n\\[\na_p+t p \\qquad (t\\ge k)\n\\]\nhas upper density $0$ for \\emph{every} residue choice $(a_p \\bmod p)_p$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe exact Round-1 gap was this: the heuristic suggested an uncovered proportion about $1/\\log x$, but there was no rigorous theorem.\n\nWe now prove a sharp partial result:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nFor any pairwise coprime sequence $(n_i)$ with $\\sum_i 1/n_i=\\infty$, and for any fixed $k\\ge 1$, \\emph{every} choice of residues gives an exceptional set of upper density $0$.\n\\end{quote}\n\nThis cleanly isolates the remaining difficulty in Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#279: the open problem is not about achieving density $0$, but about upgrading density $0$ to \\emph{finiteness}.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\n\\paragraph{Theorem.}\nLet\n\\[\n1<n_1<n_2<\\cdots\n\\]\nbe pairwise coprime positive integers with\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n_i}=\\infty.\n\\]\nFix $k\\ge 1$, and choose arbitrary residues $a_i \\pmod{n_i}$. Define\n\\[\nE_k(\\mathbf a)\n:=\n\\left\\{\nm\\in\\mathbf N :\n\\text{there do not exist } i\\ge 1 \\text{ and } t\\ge k \\text{ with } m=a_i+t n_i\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\overline d\\big(E_k(\\mathbf a)\\big)=0.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nFix $r\\ge 1$, and let\n\\[\nN_r:=n_1n_2\\cdots n_r.\n\\]\nDefine the finite-prefix avoidance set\n\\[\nB_r(\\mathbf a)\n:=\n\\{\\,m\\in\\mathbf Z : m\\not\\equiv a_i \\pmod{n_i}\\ \\text{for all } 1\\le i\\le r\\,\\}.\n\\]\nBecause the moduli $n_1,\\dots,n_r$ are pairwise coprime, the Chinese remainder theorem gives a bijection\n\\[\n\\mathbf Z/N_r\\mathbf Z \\longrightarrow\n\\mathbf Z/n_1\\mathbf Z \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathbf Z/n_r\\mathbf Z.\n\\]\nHence the proportion of residue classes modulo $N_r$ that avoid all $a_i \\pmod{n_i}$ is exactly\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^r \\left(1-\\frac{1}{n_i}\\right).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nd\\big(B_r(\\mathbf a)\\big)=\\prod_{i=1}^r \\left(1-\\frac{1}{n_i}\\right),\n\\]\nand this value is independent of the chosen residues.\n\nNow let $m\\ge (k+1)n_r$. If $m\\notin B_r(\\mathbf a)$, then for some $1\\le i\\le r$ we have\n\\[\nm\\equiv a_i\\pmod{n_i}.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nm=a_i+t n_i.\n\\]\nSince $0\\le a_i\\le n_i-1$ and $n_i\\le n_r$, we get\n\\[\nm-a_i\\ge (k+1)n_r-(n_i-1)\\ge (k+1)n_i-(n_i-1) > k n_i.\n\\]\nBecause $m-a_i$ is divisible by $n_i$, it follows that $t\\ge k$.\n\nSo every integer $m\\ge (k+1)n_r$ outside $B_r(\\mathbf a)$ is already covered by one of the first $r$ progressions with quotient at least $k$. Equivalently,\n\\[\nE_k(\\mathbf a)\\cap[(k+1)n_r,\\infty)\\subseteq B_r(\\mathbf a).\n\\]\nTaking upper densities,\n\\[\n\\overline d\\big(E_k(\\mathbf a)\\big)\\le d\\big(B_r(\\mathbf a)\\big)\n=\n\\prod_{i=1}^r \\left(1-\\frac{1}{n_i}\\right).\n\\]\nThis holds for every $r$. Since $\\sum_i 1/n_i=\\infty$, the infinite product on the right tends to $0$. Therefore\n\\[\n\\overline d\\big(E_k(\\mathbf a)\\big)=0.\n\\]\nThis proves the theorem.\n\\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary (primes).}\nTake $n_i=p_i$ to be the primes. Then for every fixed $k\\ge 1$ and \\emph{every} choice of residues $a_p \\pmod p$,\n\\[\n\\overline d\\!\\left(\n\\left\\{\nm :\nm\\neq a_p+t p \\text{ for all primes } p \\text{ and all } t\\ge k\n\\right\\}\n\\right)\n=0.\n\\]\n\nThus, in Problem \\#279, the unresolved issue is not density zero but finite exceptionality.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Threshold check.} The proof only uses the implication\n\\[\nm\\ge (k+1)n_r \\ \\text{ and }\\ m\\equiv a_i\\pmod{n_i}\n\\Longrightarrow m=a_i+t n_i \\text{ with } t\\ge k.\n\\]\nThis is valid because $a_i\\le n_i-1$.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Why only upper density?} From\n\\[\nE_k(\\mathbf a)\\cap[(k+1)n_r,\\infty)\\subseteq B_r(\\mathbf a)\n\\]\nwe get an upper-density bound. That is exactly what is needed to conclude $\\overline d(E_k(\\mathbf a))=0$.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Does this prove the original problem?} No. A set can have upper density $0$ and still be infinite. So the argument does \\emph{not} settle finiteness.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Is pairwise coprimality essential?} Yes. The exact factorisation\n\\[\nd(B_r(\\mathbf a))=\\prod_{i\\le r}(1-1/n_i)\n\\]\ncomes from the Chinese remainder theorem and can fail without pairwise coprimality.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nRound 2 proves a substantial weakening of the target statement:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nFor the primes (indeed, for any pairwise coprime sequence with divergent reciprocal sum), the exceptional set has upper density $0$ for \\emph{every} choice of residues and every fixed $k\\ge 1$.\n\\end{quote}\n\nSo Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#279 is now sharply reduced to the problem of upgrading ``upper density $0$'' to ``finite''.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\n\nCOMPLETION: 45\\%.\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item P. Erd\\H{o}s and R. L. Graham, \\emph{Old and New Problems and Results in Combinatorial Number Theory}, Monographies de L'Enseignement Math\\'ematique 28, Geneva, 1980.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n",
