          "preamble": "\\section{Erd\\H{o}s Problem 288}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\nPath \\textbf{(C)}: isolate a sharp obstruction and give an exact reformulation of the singleton subcase.\n\nWhy this direction is the right continuation of Round~1: Round~1 already had a clean one-interval non-integrality lemma and a general ``uniquely occurring prime'' obstruction, but it did \\emph{not} rigidly reformulate the still-open singleton case $|I_2|=1$, nor did it prove any structural theorem for ordered disjoint solutions. Those are the two gaps I close here.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following Round~1 results without reproving them.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item[(F1)] If $[a,b]$ is a nontrivial interval, then $\\sum_{n=a}^b \\frac1n \\notin \\mathbb Z$.\n\t\\item[(F2)] If a prime $p$ divides the common denominator to exponent exactly $1$, and among the denominators in the relevant union of intervals there is exactly one multiple of $p$, then the total reciprocal sum cannot be an integer.\n\t\\item[(F3)] The exact small solutions found in Round~1 up to endpoint $300$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThere are two genuinely new ingredients.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item[(N1)] \\textbf{Singleton forcing.} In the singleton variant\n\t\\[\n\tH(a,b)+\\frac1m\\in\\mathbb Z,\\qquad H(a,b):=\\sum_{n=a}^b \\frac1n,\n\t\\]\n\tthere is at most one possible $m$ for a fixed interval $[a,b]$: namely, $m$ must equal the reduced denominator of $H(a,b)$.\n\t\n\t\\item[(N2)] \\textbf{Terminal composite tail obstruction.} In any ordered disjoint two-interval solution\n\t\\[\n\t\\sum_{n=a}^b\\frac1n+\\sum_{n=c}^d\\frac1n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\qquad b<c,\n\t\\]\n\tthe later interval $[c,d]$ must lie entirely inside the terminal prime-free tail below $d$. In particular its length is $o(d)$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe exact unresolved gap after Round~1 was this:\n\n\\begin{quote}\n\tthere was no rigid reduction for the singleton case, and no theorem showing that the interval containing the maximal denominator must be extremely short in any ordered disjoint solution.\n\\end{quote}\n\nI therefore prove two claims.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item[(C1)] If $H(a,b)=u/v$ in lowest terms and $H(a,b)+1/m\\in\\mathbb Z$, then necessarily $m=v$.\n\t\\item[(C2)] If $b<c$ and $\\sum_{n=a}^b 1/n+\\sum_{n=c}^d 1/n\\in\\mathbb Z$, then $[c,d]$ contains no prime in $(d/2,d]$, hence in fact no prime at all, and so $d-c+1=o(d)$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThese two claims are enough to give strict progress beyond Round~1.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\n\\paragraph{Proposition 288.1 (singleton forcing).}\nLet\n\\[\nH(a,b)=\\sum_{n=a}^b\\frac1n=\\frac uv\n\\]\nwith $\\gcd(u,v)=1$ and $v>0$.\nThen\n\\[\nH(a,b)+\\frac1m\\in\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nfor some positive integer $m$ if and only if\n\\[\nu+1\\equiv 0\\pmod v,\n\\]\nand in that case necessarily $m=v$.\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nAssume\n\\[\n\\frac uv+\\frac1m=k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\frac1m=k-\\frac uv=\\frac{kv-u}{v}.\n\\]\nNow\n\\[\n\\gcd(kv-u,v)=\\gcd(u,v)=1,\n\\]\nso the fraction $(kv-u)/v$ is already in lowest terms. But $1/m$ in lowest terms has numerator $1$. Therefore\n\\[\nkv-u=1,\n\\]\nand hence necessarily\n\\[\nm=v.\n\\]\nConversely, if $u+1\\equiv 0\\pmod v$, say $u+1=kv$, then\n\\[\n\\frac uv+\\frac1v=\\frac{u+1}{v}=k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nso $m=v$ works. \\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\medskip\n\nSo the singleton subcase is equivalent to the exact congruence condition\n\\[\nH(a,b)=\\frac uv\\text{ in lowest terms and }u\\equiv -1\\pmod v.\n\\]\nFor each interval $[a,b]$ there is at most one candidate singleton.\n\n\\paragraph{Proposition 288.2 (terminal composite tail obstruction).}\nAssume\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le b<c\\le d\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=a}^b\\frac1n+\\sum_{n=c}^d\\frac1n\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nLet $p^\\star(d)$ denote the largest prime in $(d/2,d]$.\nThen\n\\[\nc>p^\\star(d).\n\\]\nConsequently $[c,d]$ contains no primes at all, and\n\\[\nd-c+1\\le d-p^\\star(d)=o(d).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nBy Bertrand's postulate there exists at least one prime in $(d/2,d]$, so $p^\\star(d)$ is well-defined for $d\\ge 2$.\n\nSuppose for contradiction that $c\\le p^\\star(d)\\le d$. Then $p^\\star(d)\\in[c,d]$.\nSince $p^\\star(d)>d/2$, there is no other multiple of $p^\\star(d)$ in $[1,d]$; indeed $2p^\\star(d)>d$.\nBecause the first interval lies entirely below $c$, the denominator $p^\\star(d)$ occurs exactly once in the union of denominators from $[a,b]\\cup[c,d]$.\nBy Round~1 foundation (F2), this forces\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=a}^b\\frac1n+\\sum_{n=c}^d\\frac1n\\notin\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\na contradiction.\nHence $c>p^\\star(d)$.\n\nNow every prime in $[c,d]$ would be $>p^\\star(d)$, contradicting maximality of $p^\\star(d)$ among primes $\\le d$. So $[c,d]$ is prime-free.\nAlso\n\\[\nd-c+1\\le d-p^\\star(d).\n\\]\nFinally, by the prime number theorem, for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and all sufficiently large $d$ there is a prime in $((1-\\varepsilon)d,d]$. Thus $p^\\star(d)>(1-\\varepsilon)d$ for large $d$, giving\n\\[\n0\\le d-p^\\star(d)<\\varepsilon d,\n\\]\nso indeed $d-p^\\star(d)=o(d)$ and hence $d-c+1=o(d)$. \\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 288.3.}\nIn any ordered disjoint solution, the interval containing the maximal denominator is a terminal block of consecutive composite numbers, of sublinear length.\n\n\\paragraph{Empirical check (clearly labeled as computation, not proof).}\nUsing exact rational arithmetic, I checked the singleton subcase up to $b\\le 1600$. The only intervals $[a,b]$ for which Proposition~288.1 succeeds are\n\\[\n[1,1],\\ [1,2],\\ [1,3],\\ [2,2],\\ [2,3],\\ [3,6],\n\\]\nwith corresponding singleton values\n\\[\nm=1,\\ 2,\\ 6,\\ 2,\\ 6,\\ 20.\n\\]\nThus up to this range the only singleton solutions are the already-known ones.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\nI now try to break the new work.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item \\textbf{Quantifier issue in Proposition~288.1.} Could there be more than one integer $k$? No. Once $u/v$ is fixed, any solution must satisfy $1/m=k-u/v>0$, so necessarily $k=\\lceil u/v\\rceil$. The proof did not require naming this $k$, but it is unique.\n\t\n\t\\item \\textbf{Hidden coprimality issue in Proposition~288.1.} The step\n\t\\[\n\t\\gcd(kv-u,v)=\\gcd(u,v)=1\n\t\\]\n\tis correct because $kv-u\\equiv -u\\pmod v$.\n\t\n\t\\item \\textbf{Scope of Proposition~288.2.} The proof uses the ordering $b<c$. It does \\emph{not} apply to overlapping solutions such as $([1,2],[1,2])$ from Round~1; this is intended.\n\t\n\t\\item \\textbf{Small-$d$ issue in Proposition~288.2.} Bertrand gives a prime in $(d/2,d]$ for every integer $d\\ge 2$, so there is no exceptional range to patch.\n\t\n\t\\item \\textbf{Does the proof really imply prime-free tail?} Yes. Once $c>p^\\star(d)$ and $p^\\star(d)$ is the largest prime in $(d/2,d]$, any prime in $[c,d]$ would be $>p^\\star(d)$ and still $\\le d$, impossible.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nWhat is new in Round~2 is rigorous, not heuristic:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item the singleton case is reduced to a \\emph{single exact candidate} $m=v(a,b)$ with a congruence condition $u(a,b)\\equiv -1\\pmod{v(a,b)}$;\n\t\\item in every ordered disjoint solution, the later interval must be a terminal composite block of length $o(d)$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThese statements are strictly stronger than the Round~1 output, but they do not settle the full problem.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE (MANDATORY)}\n\\textbf{COMPLETION: 40\\%}\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item [Er288] T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#288}, website entry, accessed 2026-03-07.\n\t\\item [ErGr80] P. Erd\\H{o}s and R. Graham, \\emph{Old and New Problems and Results in Combinatorial Number Theory}, Monographies de L'Enseignement Math\\'ematique, 1980.\n\\end{itemize}"
