          "preamble": "\\section*{Problem \\#304}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\\textbf{Path C (obstruction/correction).}\nRound~1 did not produce a rigorous theorem beyond the classical literature bounds.\nThe most promising round-2 target is therefore a sharp subproblem.\nI will do two things:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item prove a complete theorem for the prime-denominator boundary case \\((p-1)/p\\), namely that no three-term Egyptian decomposition exists for any prime \\(p\\ge 11\\);\n\\item prove that a natural reduction suggested by the Round~1 data --- namely ``the worst case should always be \\((b-1)/b\\)'' --- is already false at \\(b=79\\).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following Round~1 material.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Every rational \\(0<a/b<1\\) has some Egyptian-fraction decomposition with distinct denominators.\n\\item The exact-search framework from Round~1 computes \\(N(a,b)\\) by iterative deepening with safe pruning.\n\\item Round~1 already established the exact value \\(N(79)=6\\) with witness \\(a=77\\).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe new tool is the exact two-term criterion\n\\[\n  \\frac{u}{v}=\\frac1x+\\frac1y\n  \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n  (ux-v)(uy-v)=v^2\n\\]\nfor \\((u,v)=1\\).\nApplied to the remainder after peeling off \\(1/2\\), this turns the ``three terms?'' question for \\((p-1)/p\\) into a finite divisor congruence problem over divisors of \\(4p^2\\).\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe precise gap after Round~1 was the lack of a rigorous lower bound even for special infinite families of denominators.\nTo close that gap I will:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item prove the two-term criterion carefully;\n\\item show that any hypothetical three-term decomposition of \\((p-1)/p\\) with \\(p\\ge 11\\) must start with \\(1/2\\);\n\\item reduce the remainder to the two-term criterion and rule it out by congruence;\n\\item then use the exact-search machinery from Round~1 for one new check, namely \\(N(78,79)=5\\), in order to disprove the tempting simplification ``study only \\((b-1)/b\\)''.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\\textbf{Lemma (two-term criterion).}\nLet \\(0<u<v\\) with \\((u,v)=1\\).\nThen\n\\[\n  \\frac{u}{v}=\\frac1x+\\frac1y\n\\]\nfor some integers \\(1<x<y\\) if and only if there exists a divisor \\(d\\mid v^2\\) such that\n\\[\n  u\\mid (v+d).\n\\]\nIn that case one may take\n\\[\n  x=\\frac{v+d}{u},\n  \\qquad\n  y=\\frac{v+v^2/d}{u}.\n\\]\n\n\\medskip\n\\textit{Proof.}\nStarting from\n\\[\n  \\frac{u}{v}=\\frac1x+\\frac1y,\n\\]\nclear denominators to get\n\\[\n  uxy=v(x+y).\n\\]\nRearranging gives\n\\[\n  (ux-v)(uy-v)=v^2.\n\\]\nSo if a two-term decomposition exists, then \\(d:=ux-v\\) is a divisor of \\(v^2\\), and clearly \\(u\\mid (v+d)\\).\nConversely, assume \\(d\\mid v^2\\) and \\(u\\mid (v+d)\\), and set \\(e:=v^2/d\\).\nBecause \\((u,v)=1\\) and \\(d\\mid v^2\\), we also have \\((u,d)=1\\).\nFrom \\(d\\equiv -v\\pmod u\\) and \\(de=v^2\\) it follows that\n\\[\n  e\\equiv v^2 d^{-1}\\equiv -v\\pmod u.\n\\]\nHence both \\((v+d)/u\\) and \\((v+e)/u\\) are integers, and substituting them into\n\\[\n  (ux-v)(uy-v)=v^2\n\\]\nshows that they indeed give a two-term Egyptian decomposition.\n\\hfill \\(\\square\\)\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{Theorem.}\nLet \\(p\\ge 11\\) be prime.\nThen \\((p-1)/p\\) has no three-term Egyptian-fraction decomposition.\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n  N(p-1,p)\\ge 4\n  \\qquad (p\\ge 11\\text{ prime}).\n\\]\nTherefore the only primes with \\(N(p-1,p)\\le 3\\) are\n\\[\n  p=2,3,5,7.\n\\]\n\n\\medskip\n\\textit{Proof.}\nSuppose for contradiction that\n\\[\n  \\frac{p-1}{p}=\\frac1x+\\frac1y+\\frac1z\n\\]\nwith integers \\(1<x<y<z\\).\nIf \\(x\\ge 3\\), then\n\\[\n  \\frac1x+\\frac1y+\\frac1z\\le \\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15=\\frac{47}{60},\n\\]\nwhereas for \\(p\\ge 11\\),\n\\[\n  \\frac{p-1}{p}\\ge \\frac{10}{11}>\\frac{47}{60}.\n\\]\nSo necessarily \\(x=2\\).\nSubtracting \\(1/2\\) gives\n\\[\n  \\frac{p-2}{2p}=\\frac1y+\\frac1z.\n\\]\nNow \\(p\\) is odd, so\n\\[\n  (p-2,2p)=1.\n\\]\nApply the lemma with\n\\[\n  u=p-2,\n  \\qquad\n  v=2p.\n\\]\nThen there must exist a divisor \\(d\\mid 4p^2\\) such that\n\\[\n  p-2\\mid 2p+d.\n\\]\nEvery divisor of \\(4p^2\\) is of the form \\(2^i p^j\\) with \\(0\\le i,j\\le 2\\).\nModulo \\(p-2\\) we have \\(p\\equiv 2\\), hence\n\\[\n  d\\equiv 2^{i+j}\\pmod{p-2}.\n\\]\nTherefore the divisibility condition implies\n\\[\n  p-2\\mid 4+2^{i+j}.\n\\]\nBut \\(i+j\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\), so the right-hand side can only be one of\n\\[\n  5,\\ 6,\\ 8,\\ 12,\\ 20.\n\\]\nSince \\(p\\ge 11\\) is odd prime, \\(p-2\\) is an odd integer at least \\(9\\), and no such integer divides any of \\(5,6,8,12,20\\).\nContradiction.\nHence no three-term decomposition exists.\n\nFor the small prime exceptions we have explicit decompositions\n\\[\n  \\frac12=\\frac12,\n  \\qquad\n  \\frac23=\\frac12+\\frac16,\n\\]\n\\[\n  \\frac45=\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac1{20},\n  \\qquad\n  \\frac67=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac1{42}.\n\\]\nThus the only primes with \\(N(p-1,p)\\le 3\\) are \\(2,3,5,7\\).\n\\hfill \\(\\square\\)\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{A concrete obstruction to the ``just study \\((b-1)/b\\)'' strategy.}\nUsing the exact iterative-deepening search from Round~1, I checked that\n\\[\n  N(78,79)=5,\n\\]\nfor example via\n\\[\n  \\frac{78}{79}=\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17+\\frac1{90}+\\frac1{24885},\n\\]\nand the same exact search shows that no four-term decomposition exists.\nRound~1 already established\n\\[\n  N(77,79)=6.\n\\]\nTherefore, even at the same denominator \\(b=79\\), the extremal numerator is \\emph{not} \\(a=b-1\\).\nSo one cannot reduce Problem~\\#304 to the single subfamily \\(N(b-1,b)\\) without losing information.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Did the proof really force \\(x=2\\)?}\nYes. The bound \\(1/3+1/4+1/5=47/60\\) is the maximum possible three-term sum with \\(x\\ge 3\\), and \\((p-1)/p\\ge 10/11\\) for every prime \\(p\\ge 11\\).\n\\item \\textbf{Was the two-term criterion applied in reduced form?}\nYes. For odd prime \\(p\\), \\((p-2,2p)=1\\), so the lemma applies exactly as stated.\n\\item \\textbf{Were all divisors of \\(4p^2\\) considered?}\nYes. Every divisor is \\(2^i p^j\\) with \\(0\\le i,j\\le 2\\), so \\(i+j\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\).\n\\item \\textbf{Could the computational side remark hide a gap?}\nThe remark about \\(N(78,79)=5\\) is finite and exact: the Round~1 algorithm is an exhaustive iterative-deepening search with a safe pruning rule. It found the displayed five-term representation and found no four-term representation.\n\\item \\textbf{Does the theorem solve the original asymptotic problem?}\nNo. It solves only a prime-denominator boundary subproblem, but it is a genuine new theorem on an infinite family.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL (EXACTLY ONE)}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\nRound~2 proves the new theorem that for every prime \\(p\\ge 11\\), the fraction \\((p-1)/p\\) needs at least four unit fractions.\nEquivalently, among primes the relation \\(N(p-1,p)\\le 3\\) occurs exactly for \\(p=2,3,5,7\\).\nIn addition, Round~2 proves a concrete obstruction to the simplifying strategy of studying only \\((b-1)/b\\): already at \\(b=79\\), the true worst numerator is \\(77\\), not \\(78\\).\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\n\\textbf{COMPLETION: 27\\%}\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Round~1 output in this conversation (used as fixed foundation, including the exact value \\(N(79)=6\\)).\n\\item T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#304}.\n\\item W. van Doorn and Q. Tang, \\emph{The smallest denominator not contained in a unit fraction decomposition of \\(1\\) with fixed length}, arXiv:2512.22083.\n\\end{itemize}"
