        "raw": "\n\\section*{Round 2 continuation for Erd\\H{o}s problems \\#319, \\#359, \\#361}\n\nThis file is written as a continuation of the Round-1 write-up.  I explicitly indicate below whenever I use a Round-1 result as a black box.\n\n\\bigskip\n\\hrule\n\\bigskip\n\n\\section*{Problem \\#359}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\\textbf{Path (C): obstruction/correction.}  The Round-1 write-up still had only the trivial lower bound $a_k\\ge k$.  The most natural strict advance is therefore to prove a genuinely stronger lower bound on $a_k$, or equivalently an upper bound on the counting function\n\\[\nA(x):=\\#\\{k:\\ a_k\\le x\\}.\n\\]\nI will prove the new unconditional estimate\n\\[\nA(x)\\le \\frac{3x+3}{4},\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\na_k\\ge \\frac{4k-3}{3}.\n\\]\nThis does not settle Erd\\H{o}s' question $a_k/k\\to\\infty$, but it is a rigorous improvement over Round 1 and it rules out any positive upper density larger than $3/4$.\n\n\\subsection*{2) ROUND-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following Round-1 points as fixed background.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The original statement for general $n>1$ is internally inconsistent unless one inserts the monotonicity correction ``least integer $>a_i$''; Round 1 already made this minimal correction.\n\\item For the main asymptotic question, the case of real interest is still $n=1$.\n\\item Round 1 proved the unconditional upper bound\n\\[\na_{k+1}\\le \\binom{k+1}{2}+1.\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\n\nIn the present round I work only with the corrected $n=1$ sequence.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe new observation is that \\emph{adjacent two-term sums already force many permanent exclusions}.\n\nIf\n\\[\na_1<a_2<\\cdots<a_t\\le x/2,\n\\]\nthen the numbers\n\\[\na_1+a_2,\\ a_2+a_3,\\ \\dots,\\ a_{t-1}+a_t\n\\]\nare all distinct, all at most $x$, and none of them can ever appear later in the sequence, because each is already a sum of two consecutive earlier terms.\n\nThis gives a clean counting obstruction on $[1,x]$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe precise gap after Round 1 was the absence of any nontrivial lower bound on $a_k$ beyond $a_k\\ge k$.\n\nTo improve that, I will prove:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[(i)] for every $x\\ge 1$,\n\\[\nA(x)\\le \\frac{x+\\lceil x/2\\rceil+1}{2}\\le \\frac{3x+3}{4};\n\\]\n\\item[(ii)] equivalently,\n\\[\na_k\\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{4k-3}{3}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe mechanism is a direct ``included numbers versus permanently forbidden adjacent sums'' comparison inside the same interval $[1,x]$.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\nFor the rest of this section let\n\\[\nA(x):=\\#\\{k:\\ a_k\\le x\\}\n\\]\nfor the corrected $n=1$ MacMahon sequence.\n\n\\paragraph{Lemma 359.1.}\nFor every integer $x\\ge 1$,\n\\[\nA(x)\\le \\frac{x+\\lceil x/2\\rceil+1}{2}\\le \\frac{3x+3}{4}.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nLet\n\\[\nt:=A\\!\\left(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\right).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\na_1<a_2<\\cdots<a_t\\le \\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nConsider the $t-1$ adjacent sums\n\\[\ns_i:=a_i+a_{i+1}\\qquad (1\\le i\\le t-1).\n\\]\nBecause the sequence is strictly increasing, these sums are strictly increasing:\n\\[\ns_1<s_2<\\cdots<s_{t-1}.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\ns_i\\le 2\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\le x.\n\\]\nFinally, each $s_i$ is \\emph{not} a term of the sequence, because as soon as $a_{i+1}$ has appeared, the number $a_i+a_{i+1}$ is already representable as a sum of consecutive earlier terms, so it can never be chosen later by the defining greedy rule.\n\nHence, among the integers in $[1,x]$, there are at least $t-1$ numbers which are definitely \\emph{not} in the sequence.  Therefore\n\\[\nA(x)\\le x-(t-1)=x-t+1.\n\\]\nOn the other hand, from $\\lfloor x/2\\rfloor+1$ up to $x$ there are only\n\\[\nx-\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rfloor=\\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\navailable integers, so\n\\[\nA(x)-t\\le \\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rceil,\n\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad\nt\\ge A(x)-\\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nSubstituting this lower bound for $t$ into $A(x)\\le x-t+1$ gives\n\\[\nA(x)\\le x-\\left(A(x)-\\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rceil\\right)+1,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n2A(x)\\le x+\\left\\lceil \\frac{x}{2}\\right\\rceil+1.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nA(x)\\le \\frac{x+\\lceil x/2\\rceil+1}{2}\\le \\frac{3x+3}{4}.\n\\]\n\\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 359.2.}\nFor every $k\\ge 1$,\n\\[\na_k\\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{4k-3}{3}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nApply Lemma 359.1 with $x=a_k$.  Since $A(a_k)=k$, we obtain\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{3a_k+3}{4},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\na_k\\ge \\frac{4k-3}{3}.\n\\]\nAs $a_k$ is an integer, this yields\n\\[\na_k\\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{4k-3}{3}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\n\\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{Consequence.}\nThe counting function of the sequence has upper density at most $3/4$:\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{A(x)}{x}\\le \\frac34.\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n\\liminf_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{a_k}{k}\\ge \\frac43.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\nI check the new proof against the main possible objections.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Could two adjacent sums coincide?}  \nNo.  Since the sequence is strictly increasing,\n\\[\na_i+a_{i+1}<a_{i+1}+a_{i+2}\n\\]\nfor every valid $i$.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Could an adjacent sum later re-enter the sequence?}  \nNo.  Once $a_i$ and $a_{i+1}$ have been chosen, the value $a_i+a_{i+1}$ is permanently representable as a sum of consecutive earlier terms, so the greedy rule can never choose it later.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Did the proof use anything false for small $x$?}  \nNo.  If $t=0$ or $t=1$, the argument still gives a valid inequality because there are then $t-1\\le 0$ adjacent sums, and the final bound remains true.  In particular, $A(1)=1$ and the estimate gives $1\\le (3+1)/4$.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Is the result genuinely stronger than Round 1?}  \nYes.  Round 1 had only $a_k\\ge k$.  We now have the explicit improvement\n\\[\na_k\\ge \\frac43k+O(1).\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nThe open asymptotic questions from Erd\\H{o}s remain open, but Round 2 adds a real theorem that was not present in Round 1:\n\\[\nA(x)\\le \\frac{3x+3}{4}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\na_k\\ge \\left\\lceil \\frac{4k-3}{3}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nSo the sequence is now known to have upper density at most $3/4$, which is a genuine structural obstruction beyond the trivial $a_k\\ge k$.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 35\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#359}, \\texttt{erdosproblems.com/359} (checked in this round).\n\\item \\v{S}. Porubsk\\'y, \\emph{On MacMahon's segmented numbers and related sequences}, Nieuw Arch. Wisk. (3) 25 (1977), 403--408.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\bigskip\n\\hrule\n\\bigskip\n\n",
