          "preamble": "\\section*{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#367 --- ROUND 2}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\\textbf{Path (C): obstruction/correction.}\n\nRound-1 correctly disproved the stronger bound\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll_k n^2\n\\]\nfor every $k\\ge 3$, but it overclaimed a full resolution of the original problem. The original main question is the weaker bound\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll n^{2+o(1)},\n\\]\nwhich remains open. So the right round-2 move is to isolate the exact part that is now settled and explain the precise remaining gap.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following round-1 results as fixed input.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item For $k\\le 2$,\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll n^2.\n\\]\nIn fact, for $k=1$ one has $B_2(n)\\le n$, and for $k=2$ one has\n\\[\nB_2(n)B_2(n+1)\\le n(n+1)\\le 2n^2.\n\\]\n\\item For every $k\\ge 3$ there is an explicit infinite family $n=n_{j_t}$ such that\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ge \\prod_{n\\le m<n+3} B_2(m) \\gg n^2\\log n\n\\]\ninfinitely often.\n\\item In that family,\n\\[\nn_j=8y_j^2,\n\\]\nwhere $(x_j,y_j)$ solves $x^2-8y^2=1$, and for\n\\[\nj_t=\\frac{3\\cdot 5^{t-1}-1}{2}\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n5^t\\mid (n_{j_t}+2).\n\\]\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe genuinely new point is a \\emph{scope correction} and an \\emph{exact classification} of the stronger variant:\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll_k n^2\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le 2.\n\\]\nThis theorem was implicit in round-1 but not isolated as the minimal corrected statement. Equally important, the round-1 lower bound\n\\[\nn^2\\log n\n\\]\ndoes \\emph{not} contradict the original weaker conjecture, because $\\log n=n^{o(1)}$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe round-1 mathematical construction is already enough to settle the stronger subquestion, so I will not re-prove it. Instead I will:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item package the round-1 conclusions into the exact stronger theorem;\n\\item exhibit the first completely explicit counterexample from the family;\n\\item explain sharply why the original $n^{2+o(1)}$ problem remains open.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\\textbf{Theorem.} For fixed $k\\ge 1$, the stronger estimate\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll_k n^2\n\\]\nholds if and only if $k\\le 2$.\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{Proof.}\nIf $k=1$, then\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+1} B_2(m)=B_2(n)\\le n.\n\\]\nIf $k=2$, then\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+2} B_2(m)=B_2(n)B_2(n+1)\\le n(n+1)\\le 2n^2.\n\\]\nSo the stronger bound is true for $k\\le 2$.\n\nNow let $k\\ge 3$. By the round-1 construction, there are infinitely many $n$ such that\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+3} B_2(m) \\gg n^2\\log n.\n\\]\nTherefore for those same $n$,\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m)\n\\ge\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+3} B_2(m)\n\\gg n^2\\log n.\n\\]\nSince $\\log n\\to\\infty$, the ratio\n\\[\n\\frac{\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m)}{n^2}\n\\]\nis unbounded along that infinite sequence. Hence no constant $C_k$ can satisfy\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m)\\le C_k n^2\n\\]\nfor all large $n$. So the stronger bound fails for every $k\\ge 3$.\nThis proves the theorem.\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{First explicit counterexample from the round-1 family.}\nTake $t=2$. Then\n\\[\nj_2=\\frac{3\\cdot 5-1}{2}=7.\n\\]\nThe Pell recurrence gives\n\\[\n(x_7,y_7)=(114243,40391),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nn:=n_{j_2}=8y_7^2=13051463048.\n\\]\nDirect factorization gives\n\\[\nn=2^3\\cdot 13^4\\cdot 239^2,\n\\qquad\nn+1=114243^2=3^2\\cdot 113^2\\cdot 337^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nn+2=13051463050=2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 29\\cdot 269\\cdot 33461.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nB_2(n)=n,\n\\qquad\nB_2(n+1)=n+1,\n\\qquad\nB_2(n+2)\\ge 5^2=25.\n\\]\nSo already for this concrete $n$,\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+3} B_2(m) \\ge 25n(n+1) > 25n^2,\n\\]\nshowing explicitly that the $\\ll_3 n^2$ bound fails.\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{Why the original question is still open.}\nThe original Erd\\H{o}s problem asks whether for fixed $k$ one always has\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+k} B_2(m) \\ll n^{2+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe round-1 lower bound only shows\n\\[\n\\prod_{n\\le m<n+3} B_2(m) \\gg n^2\\log n\n\\]\ninfinitely often, and\n\\[\n\\log n=n^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nTherefore this lower bound is still compatible with an upper bound of the form $n^{2+o(1)}$. So the main conjectural barrier survives untouched.\n\nThus the stronger subquestion is now exactly classified, but the original weaker subquestion remains unresolved.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{Quantifier check:} to refute $\\ll_k n^2$ it is enough to find infinitely many $n$ with ratio to $n^2$ tending to infinity. The round-1 family gives ratio $\\gg \\log n$, which does tend to infinity.\n\\item \\emph{Main-obstruction check:} the same family does \\emph{not} refute $n^{2+o(1)}$, because $\\log n$ is swallowed by the $n^{o(1)}$ term.\n\\item \\emph{Concrete example check:} the factorization of $n$, $n+1$, and $n+2$ above confirms the mechanism already at the first nontrivial value $t=2$.\n\\item \\emph{No illicit reproving:} all deep Pell/5-adic input is used exactly as round-1 foundation; round-2 only repackages it into the correct global conclusion.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED)}\n\nThe exact stronger statement $\\ll_k n^2$ is now fully classified: true for $k\\le 2$, false for every $k\\ge 3$. But the original weaker bound $n^{2+o(1)}$ remains open.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 70\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[Bloom367] T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#367}, \\texttt{https://www.erdosproblems.com/367}, accessed 2026-03-07.\n\\item[Thread367] T. F. Bloom (ed.), \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#367 discussion thread}, \\texttt{https://www.erdosproblems.com/forum/thread/367}, accessed 2026-03-07.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\bigskip\\hrule\\bigskip"
