          "preamble": "\\section*{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#368 --- ROUND 2}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\\textbf{Path (C): obstruction/correction.}\n\nRound-1 tried to prove the baseline statement $F(n)\\to\\infty$ by a self-contained Pell/St\\o rmer argument, and it also overclaimed a full resolution of the original problem. The original problem, however, asks for \\emph{quantitative size} of $F(n)$ and remains open. Moreover, the round-1 self-contained proof has a real gap in the $p\\ge 5$ part of its uniqueness lemma. So round-2 will replace that flawed subproof by a clean theorem-level citation and correct the overall status.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI retain from round-1 only the valid reduction:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item If $F(n)\\le B$ and $S$ is the set of primes at most $B$, then both $n$ and $n+1$ are $S$-smooth.\n\\end{itemize}\nI do \\emph{not} reuse the round-1 proof of the key Pell uniqueness lemma, because that is exactly where the gap occurs.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe new tool is a precise external theorem of St\\o rmer, in the form quoted by Klazar:\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\n\\textbf{St\\o rmer's theorem.} If $S$ is a finite set of primes, then the equation\n\\[\nx-y=1\n\\]\nhas only finitely many solutions in positive $S$-numbers; in fact at most $3^{|S|}$ such solutions.\n\\medskip\n\nThis theorem exactly matches the round-1 reduction and removes the need for the flawed self-contained lemma.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe gap left after round-1 is now very precise:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item round-1 reduced $F(n)\\to\\infty$ to finiteness of consecutive $S$-smooth pairs;\n\\item round-1 then attempted a self-contained proof of that finiteness, but the contradiction in the $p\\ge 5$ case was not valid.\n\\end{itemize}\nSo I will keep the reduction and swap in St\\o rmer's theorem as a black box with verified hypotheses.\n\nThen I will explain why even the repaired statement $F(n)\\to\\infty$ is still much weaker than the original quantitative problem.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\nLet\n\\[\nF(n):=\\max\\{p : p\\mbox{ prime and } p\\mid n(n+1)\\}.\n\\]\nFix a positive integer $B$, and let $S$ be the finite set of all primes $\\le B$.\nSuppose that\n\\[\nF(n)\\le B.\n\\]\nThen every prime dividing $n(n+1)$ lies in $S$. Since any prime divisor of $n$ or $n+1$ also divides $n(n+1)$, it follows that both $n$ and $n+1$ are $S$-smooth.\n\nNow apply St\\o rmer's theorem: for a fixed finite set $S$, there are only finitely many pairs of consecutive positive $S$-smooth integers. Therefore, for this fixed $B$, there are only finitely many $n$ such that $F(n)\\le B$.\n\nBecause $B$ was arbitrary, we conclude:\nfor every $B$ there exists $N(B)$ such that\n\\[\nn\\ge N(B)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad F(n)>B.\n\\]\nThis is exactly the definition of\n\\[\nF(n)\\to\\infty\\qquad (n\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nSo the baseline statement is proved.\n\n\\medskip\n\\textbf{Why this does not solve the original Erd\\H{o}s problem.}\nThe original question is not merely whether $F(n)$ is unbounded, but how large it must be quantitatively. The current problem page records much stronger partial results, for example\n\\[\nF(n)\\gg \\frac{(\\log\\log n)^2}{\\log\\log\\log n},\n\\]\nand also records the conjectural scale\n\\[\nF(n)\\gg (\\log n)^2.\n\\]\nRound-2 proves none of these stronger quantitative statements. It only repairs the weakest baseline fact $F(n)\\to\\infty$.\n\nHence the correct global status is still unresolved.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{Hypothesis check for St\\o rmer:} the set $S=\\{p\\le B\\}$ is finite, so the theorem applies.\n\\item \\emph{Quantifier check:} for each fixed $B$ there are only finitely many $n$ with $F(n)\\le B$; this is exactly the statement $F(n)\\to\\infty$.\n\\item \\emph{Gap check:} the flawed round-1 Pell uniqueness lemma is no longer used anywhere.\n\\item \\emph{Scope check:} proving $F(n)\\to\\infty$ does not answer ``how large is $F(n)$?'' in the quantitative sense intended by the open problem.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED)}\n\nRound-2 repairs the proof of the baseline statement $F(n)\\to\\infty$ and corrects the round-1 overclaim. But the original quantitative Erd\\H{o}s problem remains open.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 40\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item[Bloom368] T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#368}, \\texttt{https://www.erdosproblems.com/368}, accessed 2026-03-07.\n\\item[Klazar] M. Klazar, \\emph{St\\o rmer's solution of the unit equation $x-y=1$}, 2010, \\texttt{https://kam.mff.cuni.cz/~klazar/stormer.pdf}.\n\\end{itemize}"
