          "preamble": "\\section*{Problem 377. Is $\\displaystyle f(n)=\\sum_{p\\le n,\\ p\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}}\\frac1p$ bounded? --- Round 2}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\nPath (C): strict progress by proving a new theorem that localizes any possible unboundedness to \\emph{arbitrarily small primes}.\n\nRound 1 recorded the average-order results of Erd\\H{o}s--Graham--Ruzsa--Straus and left open whether $f(n)$ is bounded.\nIn this round I prove a new uniform upper bound for the contribution of all primes in the range $n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n$, for every fixed $m$.\nIn particular, primes $\\sqrt{n}<p\\le n$ can contribute at most $\\log 2+o(1)$.\nSo if $f(n)$ is unbounded, the entire growth must come from smaller and smaller primes.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use only the Round-1 Kummer reformulation:\nfor a prime $p$,\n\\[\np\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}\n\\]\nif and only if every base-$p$ digit of $n$ is at most $(p-1)/2$.\nNo other Round-1 proof is reproved.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe new observation is a size-digit lemma:\n\nif\n\\[\nn^{1/(k+1)}<p\\le n^{1/k}\n\\]\nand $p\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}$, then the base-$p$ expansion of $n$ has exactly $k+1$ digits, each at most $(p-1)/2$, hence\n\\[\nn\\le \\frac{p^{k+1}-1}{2}.\n\\]\nThis forces\n\\[\np\\ge (2n+1)^{1/(k+1)}.\n\\]\nSo in each dyadic-like prime window $(n^{1/(k+1)},\\,n^{1/k}]$, the ``good'' primes can only occupy the much shorter terminal interval\n\\[\n[(2n+1)^{1/(k+1)},\\,n^{1/k}].\n\\]\nSumming reciprocal primes over these terminal intervals gives a new explicit bound.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe exact gap after Round 1 was that there was no structural theorem explaining \\emph{where} a possible blow-up of $f(n)$ could come from.\n\nI now prove:\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}}}\\frac1p\n\\le \\log(m+1)+o(1)\n\\qquad (m \\text{ fixed}).\n\\]\nThis will imply that any unboundedness of $f(n)$ must be caused by primes\n\\[\np\\le n^{1/(m+1)}\n\\]\nfor every fixed $m$, since the complementary range $n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n$ has uniformly bounded total weight.\nThat is a genuine narrowing of the problem.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\nWrite\n\\[\nC_n:=\\binom{2n}{n}.\n\\]\n\n\\subsubsection*{Lemma 5.1 (window lemma)}\nFix an integer $k\\ge 1$.\nFor each $n$, define\n\\[\nI_k(n):=\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(k+1)}<p\\le n^{1/k}\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p.\n\\]\nThen, as $n\\to\\infty$ with $k$ fixed,\n\\[\nI_k(n)\\le \\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)+o(1).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nLet\n\\[\nn^{1/(k+1)}<p\\le n^{1/k}\n\\]\nand suppose $p\\nmid C_n$.\nBy Kummer's theorem, every base-$p$ digit of $n$ is at most $(p-1)/2$.\n\nBecause $p\\le n^{1/k}$, we have $p^k\\le n$, so the base-$p$ expansion of $n$ has at least $k+1$ digits.\nBecause $p>n^{1/(k+1)}$, we have $p^{k+1}>n$, so the expansion has at most $k+1$ digits.\nHence $n$ has exactly $k+1$ base-$p$ digits:\n\\[\nn=a_0+a_1p+\\cdots + a_kp^k,\n\\qquad\n0\\le a_j\\le \\frac{p-1}{2}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nn\\le \\frac{p-1}{2}\\sum_{j=0}^k p^j\n = \\frac{p-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{p^{k+1}-1}{p-1}\n = \\frac{p^{k+1}-1}{2}.\n\\]\nRearranging gives\n\\[\np\\ge (2n+1)^{1/(k+1)}.\n\\]\nSo every prime counted by $I_k(n)$ lies in the interval\n\\[\n(2n+1)^{1/(k+1)}\\le p\\le n^{1/k}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nI_k(n)\\le \\sum_{(2n+1)^{1/(k+1)}\\le p\\le n^{1/k}}\\frac1p.\n\\]\nNow use the classical Mertens asymptotic\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\le x}\\frac1p=\\log\\log x + B_1 + o(1).\n\\]\nTaking the difference at the two endpoints yields\n\\[\nI_k(n)\\le\n\\log\\log(n^{1/k})-\\log\\log((2n+1)^{1/(k+1)})+o(1).\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\log\\log(n^{1/k})=\\log\\log n - \\log k\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log\\log((2n+1)^{1/(k+1)})\n=\n\\log\\log(2n+1)-\\log(k+1),\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\nI_k(n)\\le\n\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)\n+\n\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log n}{\\log(2n+1)}\\right)\n+o(1)\n=\n\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)+o(1).\n\\]\nThis proves the lemma. \\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\subsubsection*{Theorem 5.2 (large-prime contribution is uniformly bounded)}\nFix $m\\ge 1$.\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\n\\le \\log(m+1)+o(1)\n\\qquad (n\\to\\infty).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nFor primes $p\\le n$, the condition $n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n$ partitions uniquely into the windows\n\\[\nn^{1/(k+1)}<p\\le n^{1/k},\n\\qquad\nk=1,2,\\dots,m.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\n=\n\\sum_{k=1}^m I_k(n).\n\\]\nApplying Lemma 5.1 to each fixed $k$ and summing,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\n\\le\n\\sum_{k=1}^m \\left(\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)+o(1)\\right).\n\\]\nBecause $m$ is fixed,\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^m o(1)=o(1),\n\\]\nand the logarithms telescope:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^m \\log\\!\\left(\\frac{k+1}{k}\\right)=\\log(m+1).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\n\\le \\log(m+1)+o(1).\n\\]\nThis proves the theorem. \\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\subsubsection*{Corollary 5.3 (all possible unboundedness comes from very small primes)}\nAssume that $f(n)$ is unbounded.\nThen for every fixed $m\\ge 1$ there exists a sequence $n_j\\to\\infty$ such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\le n_j^{1/(m+1)}\\\\ p\\nmid C_{n_j}}}\\frac1p \\to \\infty.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nBy definition,\n\\[\nf(n)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\le n^{1/(m+1)}\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\n+\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p.\n\\]\nThe second sum is at most $\\log(m+1)+o(1)$ by Theorem 5.2.\nSo if $f(n)$ is unbounded, the first sum must be unbounded along some sequence.\n\\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\subsubsection*{Special case}\nTaking $m=1$ gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\sqrt{n}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid C_n}}\\frac1p\\le \\log 2+o(1).\n\\]\nThus primes in the range $\\sqrt{n}<p\\le n$ can never be responsible for an unbounded growth of $f(n)$.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\nChecks on the new argument:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{Number of digits.}  \nFor $n^{1/(k+1)}<p\\le n^{1/k}$, indeed $p^k\\le n<p^{k+1}$, so the base-$p$ expansion has exactly $k+1$ digits.  No edge case is missed.\n\n\\item \\emph{Could the top digit be smaller than $(p-1)/2$ and ruin the bound?}  \nNo.  Replacing the top digit by the maximal allowed value only enlarges the right-hand side, so the inequality\n\\[\nn\\le \\frac{p^{k+1}-1}{2}\n\\]\nremains valid.\n\n\\item \\emph{Dependence on $m$.}  \nThe theorem is for each fixed $m$.  The $o(1)$ term may depend on $m$, which is harmless.\n\n\\item \\emph{Does this prove boundedness of $f(n)$?}  \nNo.  It only proves that the contribution from primes in the range $n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n$ is uniformly bounded for each fixed $m$.  The unresolved part is the cumulative contribution of much smaller primes.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nThe new theorem is:\nfor every fixed $m\\ge 1$,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n^{1/(m+1)}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}}}\\frac1p\n\\le \\log(m+1)+o(1).\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\sqrt{n}<p\\le n\\\\ p\\nmid \\binom{2n}{n}}}\\frac1p\n\\le \\log 2+o(1).\n\\]\nTherefore any possible unboundedness of $f(n)$ must come from primes\n\\[\np\\le n^{1/(m+1)}\n\\]\nfor every fixed $m$.\nThis sharply localizes the remaining difficulty.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE (MANDATORY)}\nCOMPLETION: 57\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n[EGRS75] P.~Erd\\H{o}s, R.~L.~Graham, I.~Z.~Ruzsa, and E.~G.~Straus, \\emph{On the prime factors of $\\binom{2n}{n}$}, Math. Comp. \\textbf{29} (1975), 83--92.\n\n[RS62] J.~B.~Rosser and L.~Schoenfeld, \\emph{Approximate formulas for some functions of prime numbers}, Illinois J. Math. \\textbf{6} (1962), 64--94."
