          "preamble": "\\section{Problem \\#383 (Round 2)}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\nPath A (proof-oriented constructive progress). Round 1 had examples through $k=6$ and no example for $k=7$ in the search range used there. The new aim is to push the exact search further and replace ``none found'' by explicit rigorous constructions for larger $k$.\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\n\nI use the Round-1 reformulation:\n\nfor a prime $p>k$,\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=0}^{k}(p^2+i)\\right)=p\n\\]\nis equivalent to\n\\[\nP(p^2+i)\\le p\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nSo it is enough to exhibit primes $p$ such that each of the integers\n\\[\np^2+1,\\dots,p^2+k\n\\]\nis $p$-smooth.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe new ingredient is not a new theorem but a new exact computational certificate: I found and verified explicit primes for\n\\[\nk=7,\\ 8,\\ 9.\n\\]\nThis strictly advances Round 1, which only had examples through $k=6$.\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe exact Round-1 gap was the absence of any example for $k=7$ in the earlier search range. The new plan is:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item search much farther than Round 1;\n\\item for each successful prime $p$, give exact factorisations of $p^2+i$ for all $1\\le i\\le k$;\n\\item conclude rigorously that the largest prime factor of the whole product is exactly $p$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\n\\paragraph{Example for $k=7$.}\nTake\n\\[\np=510569.\n\\]\nThen the exact factorisations are:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $p^2+1=2\\cdot 37\\cdot 31721\\cdot 111053$;\n\\item $p^2+2=3\\cdot 2731\\cdot 4651\\cdot 6841$;\n\\item $p^2+3=2^2\\cdot 13\\cdot 43\\cdot 523\\cdot 222913$;\n\\item $p^2+4=5\\cdot 17\\cdot 29^2\\cdot 641\\cdot 5689$;\n\\item $p^2+5=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 7\\cdot 23\\cdot 167\\cdot 1061\\cdot 1523$;\n\\item $p^2+6=11\\cdot 59\\cdot 101\\cdot 991\\cdot 4013$;\n\\item $p^2+7=2^3\\cdot 71\\cdot 401\\cdot 449\\cdot 2549$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nEvery prime shown is $<p$. Since $p>7$, none of $p^2+1,\\dots,p^2+7$ is divisible by $p$. Therefore the factor $p^2$ contributes the only copies of $p$ to\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=0}^{7}(p^2+i),\n\\]\nand all other prime divisors are $<p$. Hence\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=0}^{7}(p^2+i)\\right)=p.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Example for $k=8$.}\nTake\n\\[\np=2869123.\n\\]\nThen:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $p^2+1=2\\cdot 5\\cdot 489337\\cdot 1682249$;\n\\item $p^2+2=3^6\\cdot 82129\\cdot 137491$;\n\\item $p^2+3=2^2\\cdot 7\\cdot 31\\cdot 5449\\cdot 1740451$;\n\\item $p^2+4=13\\cdot 251969\\cdot 2513089$;\n\\item $p^2+5=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 43\\cdot 563\\cdot 1321\\cdot 42901$;\n\\item $p^2+6=5\\cdot 11^2\\cdot 149\\cdot 313\\cdot 291751$;\n\\item $p^2+7=2^4\\cdot 701\\cdot 9473\\cdot 77477$;\n\\item $p^2+8=3\\cdot 953\\cdot 4003\\cdot 719281$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nAgain every displayed prime is $<p$, and $p>8$ implies $p$ does not divide $p^2+i$ for $1\\le i\\le 8$. Therefore\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=0}^{8}(p^2+i)\\right)=p.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Example for $k=9$.}\nTake\n\\[\np=3092701.\n\\]\nThen:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $p^2+1=2\\cdot 1373\\cdot 7237\\cdot 481301$;\n\\item $p^2+2=3^2\\cdot 17\\cdot 41\\cdot 89\\cdot 1289\\cdot 13291$;\n\\item $p^2+3=2^2\\cdot 73\\cdot 104947\\cdot 312121$;\n\\item $p^2+4=5\\cdot 101\\cdot 137\\cdot 1553\\cdot 89021$;\n\\item $p^2+5=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 7^2\\cdot 30781\\cdot 1056929$;\n\\item $p^2+6=11\\cdot 1709\\cdot 5021\\cdot 101333$;\n\\item $p^2+7=2^4\\cdot 919\\cdot 2417\\cdot 269131$;\n\\item $p^2+8=3\\cdot 641\\cdot 5939\\cdot 837497$;\n\\item $p^2+9=2\\cdot 5\\cdot 37\\cdot 15313\\cdot 1688161$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nAll displayed prime divisors are $<p$, and $p>9$ implies $p$ does not divide $p^2+i$ for $1\\le i\\le 9$. Hence\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=0}^{9}(p^2+i)\\right)=p.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Consequences for Problem \\#382.}\nEach of the three examples above also gives a bad interval:\n\\[\n[p^2,p^2+7],\\qquad [p^2,p^2+8],\\qquad [p^2,p^2+9].\n\\]\nSo the Problem \\#383 search now yields rigorous constructions for bad intervals of lengths $7,8,9$.\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\nI checked the key logical points.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\emph{Could one of the numbers $p^2+i$ be divisible by $p$?} No, because $1\\le i\\le k<p$ in all three examples, so $p$ does not divide $p^2+i$.\n\\item \\emph{Is it enough to factor only $p^2+1,\\dots,p^2+k$?} Yes. The factor $p^2$ already contributes the prime $p$, and once all other factors are shown to have prime divisors $<p$, the largest prime divisor of the full product is exactly $p$.\n\\item \\emph{Could there be a larger hidden prime factor in one of the listed numbers?} No. The displayed decompositions are exact factorisations.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nRound 2 replaces the Round-1 frontier\n\\[\nk\\le 6\n\\]\nby the rigorously verified frontier\n\\[\nk\\le 9,\n\\]\nthrough explicit primes for\n\\[\nk=7,\\ 8,\\ 9.\n\\]\n\nThis is substantial constructive progress toward the conjecture, but it does not prove that for every fixed $k$ there are infinitely many such primes.\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\n\nCOMPLETION: 35\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#383}, accessed 2026-03-08, \\texttt{https://www.erdosproblems.com/383}.\n\\end{enumerate}"
