          "preamble": "\\noindent\\textbf{FORMAL RESTATEMENT}\n\nLet $(n_k)_{k\\ge 1}$ be a strictly increasing sequence of nonnegative integers and let $(a_k)_{k\\ge 1}$ be nonzero complex numbers such that the power series\n\\[\nf(z)=\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k z^{n_k}\n\\]\nconverges for all $z\\in\\mathbb{C}$ (so $f$ is entire). Assume the gap condition\n\\[\n\\frac{n_k}{k}\\to\\infty\\quad\\text{as }k\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nQuestion: Must it follow that $f$ assumes every complex value infinitely often (i.e., for every $w\\in\\mathbb{C}$, the equation $f(z)=w$ has infinitely many solutions in $\\mathbb{C}$)?\n\nEdge cases: $f$ is transcendental because infinitely many terms are present. By classical Picard theory, there is at most one value $w$ that can fail to be assumed infinitely often; the conjecture asks that there is no such exceptional value.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK}\n\nThe problem statement records: Fej\\'er proved that if $\\sum 1/n_k<\\infty$ then $f$ assumes every value at least once; Biernacki proved under the same hypothesis every value is assumed infinitely often. P\\'olya proved that if $f$ has finite order then, assuming $\\limsup (n_{k+1}-n_k)=\\infty$, $f$ assumes every value infinitely often.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{ATTACK PLAN}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Proof-track idea: show that the gap condition prevents $f$ from having an exceptional value $w$ with only finitely many preimages, by ruling out representations $f-w = P e^g$.\n\\item Disproof-track idea: attempt to explicitly construct a lacunary entire function with $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ that omits a value or has only finitely many preimages for some value (i.e., build $f-w=P e^g$ with lacunary Maclaurin support).\n\\end{itemize}\nI did not complete either track.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{WORK}\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{Fast reality check (the gap condition forces unbounded successive gaps).}\nA quick check shows $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ implies $n_{k+1}-n_k$ cannot be bounded.\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{Additional computational sanity check (toy lacunary series).}\nConsider the lacunary entire function $f(z)=\\sum_{k\\ge 1} \\frac{z^{2^k}}{(2^k)!}$ (here $n_k=2^k$, so $n_k/k\\to\\infty$). Truncating at $k=4$ gives\n\\[\nP(z)=\\frac{z^2}{2!}+\\frac{z^4}{4!}+\\frac{z^8}{8!}+\\frac{z^{16}}{16!}.\n\\]\nUsing \\texttt{numpy.roots} on the degree-$16$ coefficient array, I obtained:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item degree $16$; $16$ roots in total; $2$ roots at $0$ (numerically $|z|<10^{-8}$, consistent with the factor $z^2$);\n\\item smallest nonzero root modulus $\\approx 3.3422052103$ (approximately at $\\pm 3.3422052103\\, i$);\n\\item largest root modulus $\\approx 12.2987828606$.\n\\end{itemize}\nThis does not resolve the conjecture; it only checks that a representative lacunary pattern under the hypothesis behaves nontrivially in small truncations.\n\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 1 (unbounded successive gaps).}\nIf $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ as $k\\to\\infty$, then\n\\[\n\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty} (n_{k+1}-n_k)=\\infty.\n\\]\n\n\\textit{Proof.}\nSuppose for contradiction that $n_{k+1}-n_k\\le D$ for all $k$ for some fixed $D$. Then by telescoping,\n\\[\nn_k \\le n_1 + D(k-1) \\le (n_1+D)k.\n\\]\nDividing by $k$ gives $n_k/k\\le n_1+D$, contradicting $n_k/k\\to\\infty$.\n\\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 2 (finite preimages imply an exponential-polynomial factorisation).}\nLet $F$ be a nonconstant entire function and $w\\in\\mathbb{C}$. If the equation $F(z)=w$ has only finitely many solutions in $\\mathbb{C}$ (counted without multiplicity), then there exist a nonzero polynomial $P$ and an entire function $g$ such that\n\\[\nF(z)-w = P(z) e^{g(z)}.\n\\]\n\n\\textit{Proof.}\nLet $G(z):=F(z)-w$. By assumption, $G$ is an entire function with only finitely many zeros (counted with multiplicity). Let those zeros be $\\zeta_1,\\dots,\\zeta_m$ with multiplicities $m_1,\\dots,m_m$.\nDefine the polynomial\n\\[\nP(z):=\\prod_{j=1}^m (z-\\zeta_j)^{m_j}.\n\\]\nThen $H(z):=G(z)/P(z)$ is entire and has no zeros: all zeros of $G$ have been divided out with the correct multiplicity. Since $H$ is holomorphic and nonvanishing on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb{C}$, it admits an entire logarithm: define\n\\[\n g(z):=\\int_0^z \\frac{H'(\\zeta)}{H(\\zeta)}\\,d\\zeta.\n\\]\nBecause $H'/H$ is entire (holomorphic with no poles) and $\\mathbb{C}$ is simply connected, the integral is path-independent and yields an entire function $g$ with $g'(z)=H'(z)/H(z)$. Then $(e^{-g}H)'=0$ (differentiate to check), so $H(z)=C e^{g(z)}$ for some constant $C\\neq 0$. Absorbing $C$ into $P$ (replace $P$ by $CP$) gives $G=P e^{g}$.\n\\hfill$\\square$\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{VERIFICATION}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Lemma 2 is a standard factorisation for entire functions with finitely many zeros; I verified the logarithm construction carefully: $H$ nonvanishing on simply connected $\\mathbb{C}$ implies $H'/H$ entire and integrable path-independently.\n\\item Neither lemma resolves whether such a factorisation can be compatible with lacunary Maclaurin support $\\{n_k\\}$ and the condition $n_k/k\\to\\infty$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{FINAL}\n\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED}\n\n(i) Strongest proved partial result: The gap condition $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ forces $\\limsup (n_{k+1}-n_k)=\\infty$ (Lemma 1). If $f$ had a value $w$ that is assumed only finitely often, then $f-w$ must factor as $P e^{g}$ with $P$ a polynomial and $g$ entire (Lemma 2).\n\n(ii) First gap (crisp): Show (or refute) that an entire lacunary series with $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ cannot admit a decomposition $f-w=P e^{g}$ for any $w\\in\\mathbb{C}$, where $P$ is a polynomial and $g$ is entire.\n\n(iii) Top 3 next moves:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Coefficient-obstruction attempt: derive necessary algebraic/differential recurrences for Maclaurin coefficients of $P e^{g}$ and prove these recurrences force the support to have $n_k=O(k)$ (or at least violate $n_k/k\\to\\infty$).\n\\item Computational search for structured candidates: attempt to explicitly construct (via solving for coefficients) a nonvanishing entire function with sparse support $\\{n_k\\}$, starting from a desired lacunary series and taking a convergent logarithm $g=\\log f$.\n\\item Use growth/order: combine Lemma 1 (unbounded gaps) with value-distribution theorems that rule out exceptional values for functions with sufficiently sparse exponents unless the order is finite (where P\\'olya's result applies).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n(iv) Minimal counterexample structure (if the conjecture is false): would be an entire $f(z)=\\sum a_k z^{n_k}$ with $n_k/k\\to\\infty$ for which some value $w$ has only finitely many preimages, hence $f-w=P e^{g}$; such a counterexample would amount to a nonvanishing entire function (up to a polynomial factor) whose Maclaurin series has Fabry gaps."
