        "raw": "\nLet $(A_i)$ be a family of sets with $\\lvert A_i\\rvert=\\aleph_0$ for all $i$, such that for any $i\\neq j$ we have $\\lvert A_i\\cap A_j\\rvert$ finite and $\\neq 1$. Is there a $2$-colouring of $\\cup A_i$ such that no $A_i$ is monochromatic? A problem of Komj\\'{a}th. The existence of such a $2$-colouring is sometimes known as Property B.\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{1) FORMAL RESTATEMENT}\\par\nLet $I$ be an index set and let $(A_i)_{i\\in I}$ be a family of sets with $|A_i|=\\aleph_0$ for all $i$.\nAssume that for all distinct $i,j\\in I$, the intersection $A_i\\cap A_j$ is finite and has size not equal to $1$.\n\nQuestion: does there exist a function (2-colouring)\n\\[\n c: \\bigcup_{i\\in I} A_i \\to \\{0,1\\}\n\\]\nsuch that for every $i\\in I$, the restriction $c|_{A_i}$ is not constant (equivalently, $A_i$ contains at least one point of each colour)?\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{2) QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK}\\par\nThe statement notes this is known as Property B in this setting. No further results are stated, and I do not import external literature.\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{3) ATTACK PLAN}\\par\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Proof-track idea: attempt a transfinite greedy colouring, using the fact that each $A_i$ is infinite and pairwise intersections are finite. The main difficulty is that at an uncountable stage, $A_i$ might already have many coloured points.\n\\item Disproof-track idea: search for an uncountable family with the given intersection pattern that forces one $A_i$ to become monochromatic under any 2-colouring.\n\\item Sanity check: solve the countable-indexed case explicitly.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{4) WORK}\\par\n\\textbf{Fast reality check (countable family).} If $I$ is countable (in particular finite), a direct greedy construction works and does not even need the intersection hypotheses.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 602.1 (countable family of countably infinite sets has Property B).}\\\\\nAssume $I$ is countable. Then there exists a 2-colouring of $\\bigcup_{i\\in I}A_i$ such that no $A_i$ is monochromatic.\n\n\\noindent\\emph{Proof.}\nEnumerate $I$ as $\\{i_0,i_1,i_2,\\dots\\}$.\nWe build a colouring $c$ in stages. Maintain a finite set $S_n$ of already coloured points after stage $n$, with $S_0=\\emptyset$.\n\nAt stage $n$, since $A_{i_n}$ is countably infinite and $S_n$ is finite, choose two distinct points\n\\[\nx_n, y_n \\in A_{i_n}\\setminus S_n.\n\\]\nDefine $c(x_n)=0$ and $c(y_n)=1$, and leave all other as-yet-uncoloured points undefined for now.\nSet $S_{n+1}:=S_n\\cup\\{x_n,y_n\\}$.\n\nAfter completing all stages, define $c$ on any remaining uncoloured points arbitrarily (e.g., colour them $0$).\n\nNow fix any $i=i_n$. By construction, $x_n,y_n\\in A_i$ and $c(x_n)=0$, $c(y_n)=1$. Hence $A_i$ contains both colours and is not monochromatic.\nThis holds for every $i\\in I$, so $c$ is a valid 2-colouring. \\qed\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 602.2 (transfinite recursion under ``infinitely many new points'').}\\\\\nAssume $I$ is well-ordered and that for every $i\\in I$,\n\\[\nA_i\\setminus\\bigcup_{j<i}A_j\\text{ is infinite.}\n\\]\nThen there exists a 2-colouring of $\\bigcup_{i\\in I}A_i$ such that no $A_i$ is monochromatic.\n\n\\noindent\\emph{Proof.}\nWe define a colouring by transfinite recursion along the well-order of $I$.\nSuppose colours have been assigned to all points in $\\bigcup_{j<i}A_j$.\nBy hypothesis, the set of ``new'' points\n\\[\nB_i:=A_i\\setminus\\bigcup_{j<i}A_j\n\\]\nis infinite, hence contains two distinct uncoloured points $x_i,y_i$.\nAssign $c(x_i)=0$ and $c(y_i)=1$.\nLeave any other points of $B_i$ either uncoloured until the end or colour them arbitrarily.\n\nAt the end, colour any remaining uncoloured points arbitrarily.\n\nFor each $i$, we have produced $x_i,y_i\\in A_i$ with opposite colours, so $A_i$ is not monochromatic. \\qed\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{5) VERIFICATION}\\par\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Lemma 602.1 does not use the intersection hypotheses; it works for any countable family of infinite sets.\n\\item Lemma 602.2 isolates the obstruction for uncountable families: at stage $i$ we need at least two previously uncoloured points in $A_i$, which is guaranteed if $A_i$ contributes infinitely many new elements beyond earlier sets.\n\\item In the original problem, only pairwise intersections are finite. For uncountable $I$, the union $\\bigcup_{j<i}(A_i\\cap A_j)$ can be infinite, so the ``infinitely many new points'' hypothesis of Lemma 602.2 need not follow.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{6) FINAL}\\par\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED}\n\n(i) \\emph{Strongest proved partial result.} If the index set $I$ is countable, then such a 2-colouring exists (Lemma 602.1). More generally, if the family can be well-ordered so that each $A_i$ has infinitely many new points outside the union of earlier sets, then a 2-colouring exists (Lemma 602.2).\n\n(ii) \\emph{First gap (crisp).} For an uncountable family $(A_i)_{i\\in I}$ with $|A_i|=\\aleph_0$ and pairwise intersections finite and $\\neq 1$, prove or refute the existence of a 2-colouring of $\\cup A_i$ with no monochromatic $A_i$.\n\n(iii) \\emph{Top 3 next moves.}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Attempt to show that the condition ``$|A_i\\cap A_j|\\neq 1$'' forces the existence of a well-order of $I$ satisfying the ``infinitely many new points'' hypothesis, or some weaker variant sufficient for a transfinite greedy colouring.\n\\item Search for a concrete uncountable candidate counterexample where every $A_i$ is ``almost covered'' by intersections with earlier sets in any well-order, so the greedy method cannot pick fresh points.\n\\item Finite-model sanity: experiment with finite approximations (finite sets with the same intersection pattern) to see which intersection patterns tend to obstruct 2-colourings, and whether excluding intersection size $1$ removes those obstructions.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n(iv) \\emph{What a minimal counterexample would look like.} A minimal counterexample would be an uncountable index set $I$ and countable sets $(A_i)$ with pairwise intersections finite and never of size $1$, such that for every 2-colouring $c$ of $\\cup A_i$ there exists some $i\\in I$ with $c$ constant on $A_i$. Structurally, such a family would have to defeat every attempt to assign two opposite-coloured ``fresh'' points per $A_i$.\n",
