        "raw": "\n\\section{Problem 620 (\\#620)}\n\n\\subsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\n\nPath (C): the global problem remains open, but the Round-1 lower bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge \\lfloor \\sqrt n\\rfloor\n\\]\ncan be upgraded to the best currently known lower bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge c\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n\\]\nfor some absolute constant $c>0$. This closes the exact Round-1 gap on the lower-bound side and reduces the remaining uncertainty to the polylogarithmic factor between this and the upper bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\ll \\sqrt n\\,\\log n.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\n\nI use the two Round-1 ingredients:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item if $v$ is a vertex of a $K_4$-free graph, then the neighborhood $N(v)$ induces a triangle-free graph;\n\\item the proof strategy should split according to the maximum degree $\\Delta$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nRound~1 used a greedy independent-set bound in the low-degree case. I will strengthen only that low-degree step.\n\n\\subsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\n\nThe new tool is Shearer's theorem on the independence number of sparse $K_r$-free graphs. Specialised to $r=4$, it says that for sufficiently large maximum degree $d$, every $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with maximum degree at most $d$ has an independent set of size\n\\[\n\\Omega\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\log d}{d\\log\\log d}\\right).\n\\]\n\nReplacing the Round-1 greedy bound\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)\\ge \\frac{n}{d+1}\n\\]\nby Shearer's bound upgrades the low-degree case from order $\\sqrt n$ to order\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\n\nLet $G$ be $K_4$-free on $n$ vertices, and let $\\Delta$ be its maximum degree.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item If $\\Delta$ is large, then a high-degree neighborhood $N(v)$ is already a large induced triangle-free subgraph.\n\\item If $\\Delta$ is not large, then Shearer's theorem gives a large independent set, which is itself an induced triangle-free subgraph.\n\\item Optimise the tradeoff at\n\\[\nd_\\ast=\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}.\n\\]\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThis is exactly the Round-1 degree-splitting strategy, but with a much stronger low-degree theorem.\n\n\\subsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\n\n\\paragraph{External theorem used.}\nI use the following result of Shearer.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\n\\textbf{Shearer (1995).}\nThere exists an absolute constant $c_0>0$ and an absolute threshold $D$ such that every $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices with maximum degree $d\\ge D$ has an independent set of size at least\n\\[\nc_0\\,\\frac{n\\log d}{d\\log\\log d}.\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Theorem 5.1.}\nThere exists an absolute constant $c>0$ such that every $K_4$-free graph $G$ on $n$ vertices contains an induced triangle-free subgraph on at least\n\\[\nc\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n\\]\nvertices. Equivalently,\n\\[\nf(n)=\\Omega\\!\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\\right).\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Proof.}\nLet $G$ be a $K_4$-free graph on $n$ vertices, and let $\\Delta$ be its maximum degree.\n\nSet\n\\[\nd_\\ast=\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}.\n\\]\n\nWe consider three cases.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\n\\textbf{Case 1: $\\Delta\\ge d_\\ast$.}\nChoose a vertex $v$ of degree $\\Delta$. Then $G[N(v)]$ is triangle-free by the Round-1 neighborhood observation, and\n\\[\n|N(v)|=\\Delta\\ge d_\\ast.\n\\]\nSo $G$ contains an induced triangle-free subgraph on at least $d_\\ast$ vertices.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\n\\textbf{Case 2: $D\\le \\Delta<d_\\ast$.}\nApply Shearer's theorem with $d=\\Delta$. Then $G$ has an independent set of size at least\n\\[\nc_0\\,\\frac{n\\log \\Delta}{\\Delta\\log\\log \\Delta}.\n\\]\nAn independent set is an induced triangle-free subgraph.\n\nNow the function\n\\[\n\\phi(x)=\\frac{\\log x}{x\\log\\log x}\n\\]\nis decreasing for all sufficiently large $x$, in particular for $x\\ge D$ after enlarging $D$ if necessary. Since $\\Delta<d_\\ast$, we get\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log \\Delta}{\\Delta\\log\\log \\Delta}\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\log d_\\ast}{d_\\ast\\log\\log d_\\ast}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)\\ge c_0\\,\\frac{n\\log d_\\ast}{d_\\ast\\log\\log d_\\ast}.\n\\]\n\nFor all sufficiently large $n$,\n\\[\n\\log d_\\ast\n=\n\\frac12\\log n+\\frac12\\log\\log n-\\frac12\\log\\log\\log n\n\\ge \\frac13\\log n,\n\\]\nand also\n\\[\n\\log\\log d_\\ast\\le 2\\log\\log n.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)\\ge\n\\frac{c_0}{6}\\,\n\\frac{n\\log n}{d_\\ast\\log\\log n}.\n\\]\nUsing the definition of $d_\\ast$,\n\\[\n\\frac{n\\log n}{d_\\ast\\log\\log n}\n=\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n=\nd_\\ast.\n\\]\nSo in Case~2 we again obtain an induced triangle-free subgraph of size at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c_0}{6}\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}.\n\\]\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\n\\textbf{Case 3: $\\Delta<D$.}\nThen the trivial greedy bound gives\n\\[\n\\alpha(G)\\ge \\frac{n}{D+1}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large $n$ this is much larger than $\\sqrt{n\\log n/\\log\\log n}$, since\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{D+1}\\bigg/\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n=\n\\frac{\\sqrt n}{D+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log\\log n}{\\log n}}\n\\to \\infty.\n\\]\nSo Case~3 is also stronger than the target lower bound for all large $n$.\n\nCombining the three cases, every sufficiently large $n$-vertex $K_4$-free graph contains an induced triangle-free subgraph of size at least\n\\[\nc\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n\\]\nfor some absolute constant $c>0$.\n\nBy decreasing $c$ if necessary, the same asymptotic lower bound may be stated for all $n\\ge 2$.\n\\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 5.2.}\nCombining Theorem~5.1 with the current upper bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\ll \\sqrt n\\,\\log n,\n\\]\nwe recover the currently best asymptotic window\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n\\ll f(n)\\ll \\sqrt n\\,\\log n,\n\\]\nand in particular\n\\[\nf(n)=n^{1/2+o(1)}.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The neighborhood argument is exactly the Round-1 observation: if $N(v)$ contained a triangle, adding $v$ would create a $K_4$.\n\\item Shearer's theorem is only applied in the range $\\Delta\\ge D$, where its hypotheses hold.\n\\item The monotonicity of\n\\[\n\\phi(x)=\\frac{\\log x}{x\\log\\log x}\n\\]\nis needed only for large $x$, so enlarging $D$ is harmless.\n\\item The low-degree case $\\Delta<D$ was treated separately, so there is no hidden gap when $\\log\\log \\Delta$ is small.\n\\item The conclusion is asymptotic; finite-$n$ issues are absorbed by shrinking the constant $c$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{7) FINAL}\n\n\\textbf{UNRESOLVED (BUT STRICTLY ADVANCED).}\n\nRound~1 established only the elementary bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge \\lfloor \\sqrt n\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThis round upgrades that to the best currently known lower bound\n\\[\nf(n)\\gg \\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}},\n\\]\nusing Shearer's theorem together with the same maximum-degree split.\n\nSo the remaining gap is now exactly the current polylogarithmic gap between\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{n\\log n}{\\log\\log n}}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\sqrt n\\,\\log n.\n\\]\n\n\\subsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\n\nCOMPLETION: 68\\%\n\n\\subsection*{9) REFERENCES}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item J. B. Shearer, \\emph{On the Independence Number of Sparse Graphs}, Random Structures \\& Algorithms 7 (1995), 269--271.\n\\item D. Mubayi and J. Verstra\\\"ete, \\emph{On the order of the classical Erd\\H{o}s--Rogers functions}, 2024 preprint / 2025 publication version checked.\n\\item T. F. Bloom, \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#620}, accessed 2026-03-08.\n\\item Round-1 notes from the previous iteration of this investigation (used only for the neighborhood observation and the maximum-degree splitting strategy listed in Section~2).\n\\end{itemize}\n",
