        "raw": "\\section{Erd\\H{o}s problems \\#655, \\#738, \\#740 --- Round 2 continuation}\n\n\\subsection*{Problem \\#655}\n\n\\subsubsection*{1) ROUND-2 OBJECTIVE}\nPath (C): turn the Round-1 disproof into the exact corrected statement. Round 1 already produced a regular $n$-gon counterexample, so the natural gap to close is the sharp lower bound that actually follows from the stated hypothesis.\n\n\\subsubsection*{2) Round-1 FOUNDATION USED}\nI use the following vetted Round-1 fact.\n\nThe regular $n$-gon satisfies the hypothesis of the problem and determines exactly $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ distinct distances. Therefore the claimed bound $(1+c)n/2$ is false for every fixed $c>0$.\n\nI do not re-prove that counterexample here.\n\n\\subsubsection*{3) NEW INSIGHT / TOOL (ROUND-2)}\nThe hypothesis controls the multiplicity of \\emph{each radius around a fixed point}. If one fixes a point $x_i$, then among the $n-1$ distances from $x_i$ to the other points, each distinct value can occur at most twice. Therefore $x_i$ itself determines at least $\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil$ distinct distances. Since these are all global interpoint distances, the whole configuration determines at least that many distinct distances.\n\nCombined with the Round-1 regular $n$-gon construction, this gives the exact optimum.\n\n\\subsubsection*{4) ATTACK PLAN (ROUND-2)}\nThe only remaining gap after Round 1 is to identify the best possible lower bound replacing the false inequality\n\\[\n|D|\\geq (1+c)\\frac n2.\n\\]\nI will prove the sharp correction\n\\[\n|D|\\geq \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand then use the Round-1 regular $n$-gon to show equality is attainable for every $n$.\n\n\\subsubsection*{5) WORK (ROUND-2)}\nLet $x_1,\\dots,x_n$ be distinct points in the plane satisfying the hypothesis that for every $i$ and every radius $\\rho>0$, the circle centred at $x_i$ with radius $\\rho$ contains at most two of the other points.\n\nLet\n\\[\nD:=\\{\\,|x_i-x_j|:1\\leq i<j\\leq n\\,\\}\n\\]\nbe the global distance set.\n\nFor a fixed index $i$, define the set of distances seen from $x_i$ by\n\\[\nD_i:=\\{\\,|x_i-x_j|:j\\neq i\\,\\}.\n\\]\nEvery value in $D_i$ belongs to $D$, so\n\\[\n|D|\\geq |D_i| \\qquad \\text{for every } i.\n\\]\nNow fix $i$. There are $n-1$ points other than $x_i$. By hypothesis, each radius about $x_i$ can hit at most two of them. Hence each element of $D_i$ accounts for at most two points, and therefore\n\\[\n2|D_i|\\geq n-1.\n\\]\nSo\n\\[\n|D_i|\\geq \\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil = \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nSince $|D|\\ge |D_i|$, we obtain the sharp universal lower bound\n\\[\n|D|\\geq \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nThus the exact corrected theorem is:\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Corrected theorem.}\nFor every $n\\ge 1$, every $n$-point set in the plane satisfying the stated hypothesis determines at least $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ distinct distances.\n\n\\medskip\nTo see that this is best possible, invoke the Round-1 construction: the $n$ vertices of a regular $n$-gon satisfy the hypothesis and determine exactly $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ distinct distances. Therefore\n\\[\n\\min |D| = \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nover all such $n$-point configurations.\n\n\\subsubsection*{6) ADVERSARIAL VERIFICATION}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Quantifiers.} The proof gives the lower bound for every $n\\ge 1$, not merely for large $n$.\n\\item \\textbf{Odd/even parity.} The identity $\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ handles both parity cases exactly.\n\\item \\textbf{Edge cases.} For $n=1$, the distance set is empty and $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=0$. For $n=2$, there is exactly one distance and $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=1$. The argument still works.\n\\item \\textbf{No hidden assumption.} The proof uses only the stated circle-multiplicity hypothesis; it does not assume convexity, general position, or any structure beyond distinctness of the points.\n\\item \\textbf{Sharpness.} The regular $n$-gon from Round 1 attains equality, so the correction cannot be improved.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection*{7) FINAL}\n\\textbf{FULL SOLUTION --- COUNTEREXAMPLE / DISPROOF}\n\nThe original statement is false. The exact corrected statement is:\n\\[\n|D|\\ge \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand this bound is sharp for every $n$, with equality achieved by the regular $n$-gon.\n\n\\subsubsection*{8) COMPLETION ESTIMATE}\nCOMPLETION: 100\\%\n\n\\subsubsection*{9) REFERENCES}\nNo external theorem is needed for the Round-2 correction; the proof above is self-contained.\n\n\n\\section{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem{Bloom738}\nT. F. Bloom (ed.), \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#738}, Erd\\H{o}s Problems Project, accessed 8 March 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{Bloom740}\nT. F. Bloom (ed.), \\emph{Erd\\H{o}s Problem \\#740}, Erd\\H{o}s Problems Project, accessed 8 March 2026.\n\n\\bibitem{Scott97}\nA. D. Scott, \\emph{Induced trees in graphs of large chromatic number}, Journal of Graph Theory \\textbf{24} (1997), 297--311.\n\n\\bibitem{NguyenScottSeymour24}\nT. Nguyen, A. Scott, and P. Seymour, \\emph{A note on the Gy\\'{a}rf\\'{a}s--Sumner conjecture}, Graphs and Combinatorics \\textbf{40} (2024), Article 33.\n\n\\bibitem{Rodl77}\nV. R\\\"{o}dl, \\emph{On the chromatic number of subgraphs of a given graph}, Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society \\textbf{64} (1977), 370--371.\n\n\\bibitem{KomjathShelah88}\nP. Komj\\'{a}th and S. Shelah, \\emph{Forcing constructions for uncountably chromatic graphs}, Journal of Symbolic Logic \\textbf{53} (1988), 696--707.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n",
