        "raw": "\nLet $T_2,\\ldots,T_n$ be a collection of trees such that $T_k$ has $k$ vertices. Can we always write $K_n$ as the edge disjoint union of the $T_k$? A conjecture of Gy\\'{a}rf\\'{a}s, known as the tree packing conjecture. Gy\\'{a}rf\\'{a}s and Lehel \\cite{GyLe78} proved that this holds if all but at most $2$ of the trees are stars, or if all the trees are stars or paths. Fishburn \\cite{Fi83} proved this for $n\\leq 9$. Bollob\\'{a}s \\cite{Bo83} proved that the smallest $\\lfloor n/\\sqrt{2}\\rfloor$ many trees can always be packed greedily into $K_n$. Joos, Kim, K\\\"{u}hn, and Osthus \\cite{JKKO19} proved that this conjecture holds when the trees have bounded maximum degree. Allen, B\\\"{o}ttcher, Clemens, Hladky, Piguet, and Taraz \\cite{ABCHPT21} proved that this conjecture holds when all the trees have maximum degree $\\leq c\\frac{n}{\\log n}$ for some constant $c>0$. Janzer and Montgomery \\cite{JaMo24} have proved that there exists some $c>0$ such that the largest $cn$ trees can be packed into $K_n$. References [ABCHPT21] Allen, Peter and B\\\"ottcher, Julia and Clemens, Dennis and Hladky, J. and D. Piguet and Taraz, Anusch, The tree packing conjecture for trees of almost linear maximum degree . arXiv:2106.11720 (2021). [Bo83] Bollob\\'{a}s, B\\'{e}la, Some remarks on packing trees . Discrete Math. (1983), 203-204. [Fi83] Fishburn, P. C., Balanced integer arrays: a matrix packing theorem . J. Combin. Theory Ser. A (1983), 98-101. [GyLe78] Gy\\'{a}rf\\'{a}s, A. and Lehel, J., Packing trees of different order into {$K\\sb{n}$} . (1978), 463-469. [JKKO19] Joos, Felix and Kim, Jaehoon and K\\\"{u}hn, Daniela and Osthus, Deryk, Optimal packings of bounded degree trees . J. Eur. Math. Soc. (JEMS) (2019), 3573-3647. [JaMo24] Janzer, B. and R. Montgomery, Packing the largest trees in the tree packing conjecture . arXiv:2403.10515 (2024).\n\n\n\\noindent\\textbf{1) FORMAL RESTATEMENT.}\n\nFix an integer $n\\ge 2$. Let $T_2,\\dots,T_n$ be trees (simple graphs) such that $|V(T_k)|=k$ for each $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$.\n\nWe ask whether for every such collection there exist injective maps\n\\[\\varphi_k:V(T_k)\\hookrightarrow [n]\\]\nso that the image edge sets\n\\[\\varphi_k(E(T_k)):=\\{\\{\\varphi_k(u),\\varphi_k(v)\\}:\\{u,v\\}\\in E(T_k)\\}\n\\subseteq E(K_n)\n\\]\nare pairwise disjoint and their union equals $E(K_n)$.\n\nEquivalently: can $K_n$ be decomposed into edge-disjoint subgraphs isomorphic to $T_k$ for $k=2,\\dots,n$?\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{2) QUICK LITERATURE/CONTEXT CHECK.}\n\nThe problem statement lists multiple partial results (special families of trees, small $n$, bounded degree, etc.). I do not use any external results beyond what is explicitly stated there; in particular, below I give an explicit proof for the ``all stars'' special case and an exhaustive computational sanity check for $n\\le 8$.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{3) ATTACK PLAN.}\n\n\\emph{Proof-track ideas.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Induction on $n$ by embedding the largest tree $T_n$ and then decomposing the leftover graph into the remaining trees (hard because the leftover graph need not be complete).\n\\item Explicit constructions for restricted families (stars, paths) as base cases.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\emph{Disproof-track ideas.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Try to construct a collection of trees whose degree constraints force too many edges incident to a single vertex, preventing an edge-disjoint embedding.\n\\end{itemize}\nIn this write-up: prove two basic structural lemmas and provide exhaustive checks for small $n$.\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{4) WORK.}\n\n\\noindent\\emph{Fast reality check (computation).}\nI exhaustively verified the conjectured packing property for all choices of trees up to isomorphism for $n\\le 8$.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item For each $k$, I generated all nonisomorphic trees on $k$ vertices (counts: $1,1,2,3,6,11,23$ for $k=2,3,4,5,6,7,8$).\n\\item For each sequence $(T_2,\\dots,T_n)$ of such types (there are $1,1,2,6,36,396,9108$ sequences for $n=2,3,4,5,6,7,8$), I used backtracking to search for edge-disjoint embeddings into $K_n$.\n\\item Result: every sequence for $n\\le 8$ admitted a packing (no counterexample found).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 743.1 (Edge count matches exactly).}\nFor any $n\\ge 2$,\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=2}^n |E(T_k)| = \\sum_{k=2}^n (k-1) = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = |E(K_n)|.\n\\]\nIn particular, if the $T_k$ can be embedded edge-disjointly into $K_n$, then they necessarily form a \\emph{decomposition} of $K_n$ (no leftover edges).\n\n\\noindent\\emph{Proof.}\nA tree on $k$ vertices has exactly $k-1$ edges. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=2}^n |E(T_k)|=\\sum_{k=2}^n (k-1)=\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} j = \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\nAlso $K_n$ has $\\binom{n}{2}=n(n-1)/2$ edges, giving the claimed equality. \\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{Lemma 743.2 (Explicit packing when all $T_k$ are stars).}\nSuppose for each $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$, the tree $T_k$ is a star on $k$ vertices (i.e., $K_{1,k-1}$). Then $K_n$ can be written as an edge-disjoint union of the $T_k$.\n\n\\noindent\\emph{Proof.}\nLabel the vertices of $K_n$ as $1,2,\\dots,n$.\nFor each $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,n\\}$, define a star subgraph $S_k$ centered at vertex $k$ with leaf set $\\{1,2,\\dots,k-1\\}$, i.e.\n\\[\nE(S_k)=\\{\\{k,i\\}: 1\\le i\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\nThen $S_k$ has $k-1$ edges and is isomorphic to $K_{1,k-1}$.\n\nThe edge sets $E(S_k)$ are pairwise disjoint: if an edge $\\{a,b\\}$ has $a<b$, then it appears exactly in $E(S_b)$ and in no other $E(S_k)$.\n\nFinally, every edge $\\{a,b\\}$ of $K_n$ with $a<b$ belongs to $E(S_b)$, so the union of these stars covers all edges. Thus the $S_k$ form an edge-disjoint decomposition of $K_n$ into the desired stars. \\hfill $\\square$\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{5) VERIFICATION.}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item Lemma~743.1: checked the arithmetic sum $\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} j=n(n-1)/2$.\n\\item Lemma~743.2: verified disjointness carefully by unique assignment of edge $\\{a,b\\}$ to the star centered at $\\max\\{a,b\\}$.\n\\item Computation: the search explicitly constructed embeddings, so a reported ``True'' means a certificate packing exists for that instance.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\medskip\n\\noindent\\textbf{6) FINAL.} \\textbf{UNRESOLVED}\n\n(i) Strongest proved partial result: a complete explicit construction when all trees are stars (Lemma~743.2), plus exhaustive verification for all isomorphism-type sequences for $n\\le 8$.\n\n(ii) First gap: prove (or disprove via an explicit counterexample) that for every $n$ and every collection of trees $T_2,\\dots,T_n$ with $|V(T_k)|=k$, there is an edge-disjoint packing into $K_n$.\n\n(iii) Top 3 next moves:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Attempt an induction scheme that embeds the largest tree $T_n$ in a way that leaves a highly structured remainder graph (e.g., with large minimum degree), enabling recursive packing of $T_2,\\dots,T_{n-1}$.\n\\item Develop a robust greedy/random embedding lemma for packing $T_n,T_{n-1},\\dots$ while maintaining quasirandomness of unused edges.\n\\item Search computationally for small counterexample structures by restricting to ``extremal'' tree shapes (e.g., high maximum degree vs. long paths) and testing $n=9,10,\\dots$ beyond exhaustive limits.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n(iv) Minimal counterexample structure (if false): a smallest $n$ and a sequence $T_2,\\dots,T_n$ such that no decomposition exists. By minimality, every proper subsequence would pack in the corresponding smaller complete graph. Such a counterexample would likely involve a few trees forcing many edges incident to the same vertex in any embedding (degree-saturation obstruction).\n\n\n",
